  "stigs": [
    {
      "stig_name": "Database Security Requirements Guide",
      "display_name": "Database Security Requirements Guide",
      "stig_id": "Database_Generic",
      "version": "4",
      "release_info": "Release: 6 Benchmark Date: 30 Sep 2026",
      "uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
      "reference_identifier": "2902",
      "size": 142,
      "rules": [
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206519",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206519",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000001",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206519",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000001",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206519r960735_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206519r960735",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000001-DB-000031",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must limit the number of concurrent sessions to an organization-defined number per user for all accounts and/or account types.",
          "fix_text": "If the DBMS is capable of enforcing this restriction, but is not configured to do so, configure it to do so. (This may involve the development of one or more triggers.)\n\nIf it is not technically feasible for the DBMS to enforce this restriction, and the application(s) and supporting software are not configured to do so, configure them to do so.\n\nIf the value for any type of user account is not set, determine the correct value and set it. \n\nIf a value is set but is not equal to the value specified for the type of user, determine the correct value, set it, and update the documentation, as appropriate.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Determine whether the system documentation specifies limits on the number of concurrent DBMS sessions per account by type of user. If it does not, assume a limit of 10 for database administrators and 2 for all other users.\n\nReview the concurrent-sessions settings in the DBMS and/or the applications using it, and/or the system software supporting it. \n\nIf the DBMS is capable of enforcing this restriction but is not configured to do so, this is a finding. This holds even if the restriction is enforced by applications or supporting software.\n\nIf it is not technically feasible for the DBMS to enforce this restriction, but the application(s) or supporting software are configured to do so, this is not a finding. \n\nIf it is not technically feasible for the DBMS to enforce this restriction, and applications and supporting software are not so configured, this is a finding.\n\nIf the value for any type of user account is not set, this is a finding.\n\nIf a value is set but is not equal to the value specified in the documentation (or the default value defined in this check) for the type of user, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Database management includes the ability to control the number of users and user sessions utilizing a DBMS. Unlimited concurrent connections to the DBMS could allow a successful Denial of Service (DoS) attack by exhausting connection resources; and a system can also fail or be degraded by an overload of legitimate users. Limiting the number of concurrent sessions per user is helpful in reducing these risks.\n\nThis requirement addresses concurrent session control for a single account. It does not address concurrent sessions by a single user via multiple system accounts; and it does not deal with the total number of sessions across all accounts.\n\nThe capability to limit the number of concurrent sessions per user must be configured in or added to the DBMS (for example, by use of a logon trigger), when this is technically feasible. Note that it is not sufficient to limit sessions via a web server or application server alone, because legitimate users and adversaries can potentially connect to the DBMS by other means.\n\nThe organization will need to define the maximum number of concurrent sessions by account type, by account, or a combination thereof. In deciding on the appropriate number, it is important to consider the work requirements of the various types of users. For example, 2 might be an acceptable limit for general users accessing the database via an application; but 10 might be too few for a database administrator using a database management GUI tool, where each query tab and navigation pane may count as a separate session.\n\n(Sessions may also be referred to as connections or logons, which for the purposes of this requirement are synonyms.)",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42474",
            "V-32157"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000054"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "fcdcb009-1079-5fcf-b6fe-f20619a37f7f",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Saved PostgreSQL role provisioning does not set a per-role connection limit, while application pooling and shared IdP login-session limits do not establish native database enforcement. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work: verify effective role limits and define approved limits by account type, including service-pool requirements, then enforce missing limits in PostgreSQL. The official check assumes 10 administrator and 2 other-user sessions if documentation specifies none. The proposed one-browser-session policy must not be applied blindly to database service roles.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, inspect effective PostgreSQL role connection limits and identify all relevant account types and enforcement limitations. Obtain documented organization-approved administrator/service/user limits sized for actual pool and operational needs, applying the official defaults only where documentation is absent. Configure missing native limits where technically enforceable, verify behavior and retain policy/configuration/test evidence. Do not rely on application pooling alone or choose an arbitrary service-role maximum.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-query-context] re-database-query-context\nAPI service uses database pool with12-second statement_timeout and logs startup/close. This limits query duration, not DB per-role concurrent sessions or all connection idle lifetime.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/database/database.service.ts\n\n[re-database-pg-roles] re-database-pg-roles\nPostgreSQL roles support per-role connection limits and password expiration; unset lifetime/connection limits are not inherited from Keycloak.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createrole.html",
          "finding_details": "Saved PostgreSQL role provisioning does not set a per-role connection limit, while application pooling and shared IdP login-session limits do not establish native database enforcement. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work: verify effective role limits and define approved limits by account type, including service-pool requirements, then enforce missing limits in PostgreSQL. The official check assumes 10 administrator and 2 other-user sessions if documentation specifies none. The proposed one-browser-session policy must not be applied blindly to database service roles.\n\nNext action: In the post-classification verification pass, inspect effective PostgreSQL role connection limits and identify all relevant account types and enforcement limitations. Obtain documented organization-approved administrator/service/user limits sized for actual pool and operational needs, applying the official defaults only where documentation is absent. Configure missing native limits where technically enforceable, verify behavior and retain policy/configuration/test evidence. Do not rely on application pooling alone or choose an arbitrary service-role maximum."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206520",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206520",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000023",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206520",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000023",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206520r1043176_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206520r1043176",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000023-DB-000001",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must integrate with an organization-level authentication/access mechanism providing account management and automation for all users, groups, roles, and any other principals.",
          "fix_text": "Integrate DBMS security with an organization-level authentication/access mechanism providing account management for all users, groups, roles, and any other principals.\n\nFor each DBMS-managed account that is not documented and approved, either transfer it to management by the external mechanism, or document the need for it and obtain approval, as appropriate.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If all accounts are authenticated by the organization-level authentication/access mechanism and not by the DBMS, this is not a finding.\n\nIf there are any accounts managed by the DBMS, review the system documentation for justification and approval of these accounts.\n\nIf any DBMS-managed accounts exist that are not documented and approved, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Enterprise environments make account management for applications and databases challenging and complex. A manual process for account management functions adds the risk of a potential oversight or other error. Managing accounts for the same person in multiple places is inefficient and prone to problems with consistency and synchronization.\n\nA comprehensive application account management process that includes automation helps to ensure that accounts designated as requiring attention are consistently and promptly addressed. \n\nExamples include, but are not limited to, using automation to take action on multiple accounts designated as inactive, suspended, or terminated, or by disabling accounts located in non-centralized account stores, such as multiple servers. Account management functions can also include: assignment of group or role membership; identifying account type; specifying user access authorizations (i.e., privileges); account removal, update, or termination; and administrative alerts. The use of automated mechanisms can include, for example: using email or text messaging to notify account managers when users are terminated or transferred; using the information system to monitor account usage; and using automated telephone notification to report atypical system account usage.\n\nThe DBMS must be configured to automatically utilize organization-level account management functions, and these functions must immediately enforce the organization's current account policy. \n\nAutomation may be comprised of differing technologies that when placed together contain an overall mechanism supporting an organization's automated account management requirements.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42509",
            "V-32192"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000015"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "c95073ab-5970-5f9c-82dd-a1a646ae0eb4",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Native accounts are explicitly documented deployment/service roles in package Secrets and migration code; human application users are managed by the selected shared UDS identity provider, not independent SQL user accounts. The official check allows justified/approved DBMS-managed accounts, so a second directory connector in PostgreSQL is not inherently required.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the documented service-account inventory and approval with inherited human administration controls; investigate any separately added human SQL accounts.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-machine-secrets] re-database-machine-secrets\nService/admin DB passwords generated with openssl rand -hex24 when not supplied, retained from existing Secrets on upgrade; app/openfga/admin are explicit deployment identities, not per-human accounts.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Native accounts are explicitly documented deployment/service roles in package Secrets and migration code; human application users are managed by the selected shared UDS identity provider, not independent SQL user accounts. The official check allows justified/approved DBMS-managed accounts, so a second directory connector in PostgreSQL is not inherently required.\n\nNext action: Retain the documented service-account inventory and approval with inherited human administration controls; investigate any separately added human SQL accounts."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206521",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206521",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000033",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206521",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000033",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206521r1268161_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206521r1268161",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000033-DB-000084",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must enforce approved authorizations for logical access to information and system resources in accordance with applicable access control policies.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS settings and access controls to permit user access only to objects and data that the user is authorized to view or interact with, and to prevent access to all other objects and data.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS settings to determine whether users are restricted from accessing objects and data they are not authorized to access.\n\nIf appropriate access controls are not implemented to restrict access to authorized users and to restrict the access of those users to objects and data they are authorized to see, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Authentication with a DoW-approved PKI certificate does not necessarily imply authorization to access the DBMS. To mitigate the risk of unauthorized access to sensitive information by entities that have been issued certificates by DoW-approved PKIs, all DoW systems, including databases, must be properly configured to implement access control policies. \n\nSuccessful authentication must not automatically give an entity access to an asset or security boundary. Authorization procedures and controls must be implemented to ensure each authenticated entity also has a validated and current authorization. Authorization is the process of determining whether an entity, once authenticated, is permitted to access a specific asset. Information systems use access control policies and enforcement mechanisms to implement this requirement. \n\nAccess control policies include identity-based policies, role-based policies, and attribute-based policies. Access enforcement mechanisms include access control lists, access control matrices, and cryptography. These policies and mechanisms must be employed by the application to control access between users (or processes acting on behalf of users) and objects (e.g., devices, files, records, processes, programs, and domains) in the information system. \n\nThis requirement is applicable to access control enforcement applications, a category that includes database management systems. If the DBMS does not follow applicable policy when approving access, it may be in conflict with networks or other applications in the information system. This may result in users either gaining or being denied access inappropriately and in conflict with applicable policy.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42520",
            "V-32203"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000213"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "32d490dc-156a-5218-abd2-d2b81c0d3a12",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206522",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206522",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000080",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206522",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000080",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206522r960864_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206522r960864",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000080-DB-000063",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must protect against a user falsely repudiating having performed organization-defined actions.",
          "fix_text": "Use accounts assigned to individual users. Where the application connects to the DBMS using a standard, shared account, ensure that it also captures the individual user identification and passes it to the DBMS.\n\nModify application database tables and all supporting code to capture the necessary audit data.\n\nModify the configuration of audit logs to include details identifying the individual user.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system documentation to determine the data and the actions on data that need to be protected from repudiation by means of audit trails.\n\nReview DBMS settings to determine whether users can be identified as individuals when using shared accounts. If the individual user who is using a shared account cannot be identified, this is a finding.\n\nReview the design and the contents of the application data tables. If they do not include the necessary audit data, this is a finding.\n\nReview the configuration of audit logs to determine whether auditing includes details identifying the individual user. If it does not, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Non-repudiation of actions taken is required in order to maintain data integrity. Examples of particular actions taken by individuals include creating information, sending a message, approving information (e.g., indicating concurrence or signing a contract), and receiving a message. \n\nNon-repudiation protects against later claims by a user of not having created, modified, or deleted a particular data item or collection of data in the database.\n\nIn designing a database, the organization must define the types of data and the user actions that must be protected from repudiation. The implementation must then include building audit features into the application data tables and configuring the DBMS's audit tools to capture the necessary audit trail. Design and implementation also must ensure that applications pass individual user identification to the DBMS, even where the application connects to the DBMS with a standard, shared account.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42684",
            "V-32347"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000166"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "252ca76b-5c99-51ce-b6a8-2d477cea87b5",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The product supplies individual attribution through transaction-local tenant/user context, append-only permission grant/revoke records and database-triggered feature-flag audit records. These meet nonrepudiation for those defined actions without forcing every record through pgAudit. Separate native/admin audit-event gaps remain tracked rather than declaring all product nonrepudiation absent.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Map the approved protected actions to these existing records and the shared DB/admin audit event workstream.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-cls] re-database-cls\nAuthenticated tenant and user are set transaction-locally as app.tenant_id/app.user_id. Pool reuse does not retain another request identity.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/cls/cls.adapter.ts\n\n[re-database-permission-audit] re-database-permission-audit\nAppend-only role-change records carry tenant, individual actor/subject, grant/revoke action and timestamp; database-specific admin auditing remains distinct.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-flag-audit] re-database-flag-audit\nSecurity-definer feature flag audit triggers record actor from app.user_id, action/old/new values and reason; demonstrates application/database-trigger auditing can meet a defined event requirement without pgAudit for that event.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/feature_flag_functions.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The product supplies individual attribution through transaction-local tenant/user context, append-only permission grant/revoke records and database-triggered feature-flag audit records. These meet nonrepudiation for those defined actions without forcing every record through pgAudit. Separate native/admin audit-event gaps remain tracked rather than declaring all product nonrepudiation absent.\n\nNext action: Map the approved protected actions to these existing records and the shared DB/admin audit event workstream."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206523",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206523",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000089",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206523",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000089",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206523r1268163_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206523r1268163",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000089-DB-000064",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must provide audit record generation capability for DoW-defined auditable events within all DBMS/database components.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS that supports the DoW minimum set of auditable events.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to generate audit records for at least the DoW minimum set of events.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS auditing to determine whether organization-defined auditable events are being audited by the system.\n\nIf organization-defined auditable events are not being audited, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without the capability to generate audit records, it would be difficult to establish, correlate, and investigate the events relating to an incident or identify those responsible for one. \n\nAudit records can be generated from various components within the DBMS (e.g., process, module). Certain specific application functionalities may be audited as well. The list of audited events is the set of events for which audits are to be generated. This set of events is typically a subset of the list of all events for which the system is capable of generating audit records.\n\nDoW has defined the list of events for which the DBMS will provide an audit record generation capability as the following: \n\n(i) Successful and unsuccessful attempts to access, modify, or delete privileges, security objects, security levels, or categories of information (e.g., classification levels);\n\n(ii) Access actions, such as successful and unsuccessful logon attempts, privileged activities, or other system-level access, starting and ending time for user access to the system, concurrent logons from different workstations, successful and unsuccessful accesses to objects, all program initiations, and all direct access to the information system; and\n\n(iii) All account creation, modification, disabling, and termination actions.\n\nOrganizations may define additional events requiring continuous or ad hoc auditing.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42699",
            "V-32362"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000169"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "0f666dd7-21f5-5a87-8827-1c80c95e327b",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered code preloads pgAudit but does not select session event classes or enable successful native connection/disconnection records. Vector centralizes emitted logs; optional SQL tracing defaults off and cannot account for direct administrator connections. Therefore the specific required native DB event coverage remains a real shared configuration gap, despite existing product-level audit mechanisms.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Enable the required native connection/disconnection/privileged/direct-access event selection and contextual fields, with data/performance/redaction review; one shared audit workstream covers these rows.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-extension] re-database-extension\nMigration creates pgAudit extension but catches unavailable errors; no session/object audit event settings are enabled by this migration.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[re-database-tracing] re-database-tracing\nOptional SQL tracing carries parameterized query identity, db.namespace and outcome, excluding bind values. Config defaults tracing off, so it is not an enabled universal SQL audit equivalent.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/database-telemetry.ts\n\n[re-database-config] re-database-config\nOpenTelemetry tracing defaults false, and SQL SSL default disabled under intended ambient mesh. No native DB certificate-auth/cache mechanism is configured.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/config/config.schema.ts\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-database-pg-log] re-database-pg-log\nPostgreSQL emits server errors to stderr by default; ERROR severity identifies failed/denied SQL and log_min_error_statement includes the failing command. Successful connection/disconnection logging requires explicit settings.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "The delivered code preloads pgAudit but does not select session event classes or enable successful native connection/disconnection records. Vector centralizes emitted logs; optional SQL tracing defaults off and cannot account for direct administrator connections. Therefore the specific required native DB event coverage remains a real shared configuration gap, despite existing product-level audit mechanisms.\n\nNext action: Enable the required native connection/disconnection/privileged/direct-access event selection and contextual fields, with data/performance/redaction review; one shared audit workstream covers these rows."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206524",
