          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000099",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206532r960903_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206532r960903",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000099-DB-000043",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must produce audit records containing sufficient information to establish the outcome (success or failure) of the events.",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS audit settings to include the outcome of the event as part of the audit record.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS settings and existing audit records to verify information specific to the outcome of the event is being captured and stored with the audit records.\n\nIf audit records exist without the outcome of the event that occurred, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information system auditing capability is critical for accurate forensic analysis. Without information about the outcome of events, security personnel cannot make an accurate assessment as to whether an attack was successful or if changes were made to the security state of the system.\n\nEvent outcomes can include indicators of event success or failure and event-specific results (e.g., the security state of the information system after the event occurred). As such, they also provide a means to measure the impact of an event and help authorized personnel to determine the appropriate response.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42710",
            "V-32373"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000134"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "a51a0d84-b3b1-56c1-924c-18560e239a4d",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL ERROR records identify failed/denied commands; successful application audit records are written transactionally only when the change succeeds. Optional telemetry also has explicit success/error outcome but is not assumed enabled. These existing sources establish outcome semantics; expanding event coverage is separately tracked.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain this source and delivered-release mapping with the component checklist; confirm applicability and accepted inheritance at sign-off.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-pg-log] re-database-pg-log\nPostgreSQL emits server errors to stderr by default; ERROR severity identifies failed/denied SQL and log_min_error_statement includes the failing command. Successful connection/disconnection logging requires explicit settings.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[re-database-permission-audit] re-database-permission-audit\nAppend-only role-change records carry tenant, individual actor/subject, grant/revoke action and timestamp; database-specific admin auditing remains distinct.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-flag-audit] re-database-flag-audit\nSecurity-definer feature flag audit triggers record actor from app.user_id, action/old/new values and reason; demonstrates application/database-trigger auditing can meet a defined event requirement without pgAudit for that event.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/feature_flag_functions.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-tracing] re-database-tracing\nOptional SQL tracing carries parameterized query identity, db.namespace and outcome, excluding bind values. Config defaults tracing off, so it is not an enabled universal SQL audit equivalent.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/database-telemetry.ts\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL ERROR records identify failed/denied commands; successful application audit records are written transactionally only when the change succeeds. Optional telemetry also has explicit success/error outcome but is not assumed enabled. These existing sources establish outcome semantics; expanding event coverage is separately tracked.\n\nNext action: Retain this source and delivered-release mapping with the component checklist; confirm applicability and accepted inheritance at sign-off."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206533",
