          "uuid": "67c82890-1bcd-52c0-b4fb-a8955485de70",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Collector labels identify the DB container but do not identify every original native SQL caller. Default native prefix is not configured with complete user/database/remote/session context; application actor-aware records cover only selected actions. Complete native audit origin/identity context is still missing for required DB/admin operations.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Configure required native session/user/database/remote context and correlate application actor where shared service roles are used.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-pg-log] re-database-pg-log\nPostgreSQL emits server errors to stderr by default; ERROR severity identifies failed/denied SQL and log_min_error_statement includes the failing command. Successful connection/disconnection logging requires explicit settings.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[re-database-cls] re-database-cls\nAuthenticated tenant and user are set transaction-locally as app.tenant_id/app.user_id. Pool reuse does not retain another request identity.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/cls/cls.adapter.ts\n\n[re-database-permission-audit] re-database-permission-audit\nAppend-only role-change records carry tenant, individual actor/subject, grant/revoke action and timestamp; database-specific admin auditing remains distinct.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Collector labels identify the DB container but do not identify every original native SQL caller. Default native prefix is not configured with complete user/database/remote/session context; application actor-aware records cover only selected actions. Complete native audit origin/identity context is still missing for required DB/admin operations.\n\nNext action: Configure required native session/user/database/remote context and correlate application actor where shared service roles are used."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206534",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206534",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000101",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206534",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000101",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206534r960909_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206534r960909",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000101-DB-000044",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must include additional, more detailed, organization-defined information in the audit records for audit events identified by type, location, or subject.",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS audit settings to include all organization-defined detailed information in the audit records for audit events identified by type, location, or subject.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the system documentation to identify what additional information the organization has determined to be necessary.\n\nCheck DBMS settings and existing audit records to verify that all organization-defined additional, more detailed information is in the audit records for audit events identified by type, location, or subject.\n\nIf any additional information is defined and is not contained in the audit records, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information system auditing capability is critical for accurate forensic analysis. Reconstruction of harmful events or forensic analysis is not possible if audit records do not contain enough information. To support analysis, some types of events will need information to be logged that exceeds the basic requirements of event type, time stamps, location, source, outcome, and user identity. If additional information is not available, it could negatively impact forensic investigations into user actions or other malicious events.\n\nThe organization must determine what additional information is required for complete analysis of the audited events. The additional information required is dependent on the type of information (e.g., sensitivity of the data and the environment within which it resides). At a minimum, the organization must employ either full-text recording of privileged commands or the individual identities of users of shared accounts, or both. The organization must maintain audit trails in sufficient detail to reconstruct events to determine the cause and impact of compromise. \n\nExamples of detailed information the organization may require in audit records are full-text recording of privileged commands or the individual identities of shared account users.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42712",
            "V-32375"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000135"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "721eff51-c54e-5ecb-81db-c69216d32ff5",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The official check fails only when organization-defined additional audit information has been specified and is absent. No extra-field catalog was supplied; existing actor/tenant/action/time records are not automatically noncompliant merely because the app does not log arbitrary additional data.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record the required additional fields or accepted no-additional-fields applicability; implement any identified deltas in the shared audit workstream.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-permission-audit] re-database-permission-audit\nAppend-only role-change records carry tenant, individual actor/subject, grant/revoke action and timestamp; database-specific admin auditing remains distinct.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-flag-audit] re-database-flag-audit\nSecurity-definer feature flag audit triggers record actor from app.user_id, action/old/new values and reason; demonstrates application/database-trigger auditing can meet a defined event requirement without pgAudit for that event.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/feature_flag_functions.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. The official check fails only when organization-defined additional audit information has been specified and is absent. No extra-field catalog was supplied; existing actor/tenant/action/time records are not automatically noncompliant merely because the app does not log arbitrary additional data.\n\nNext action: Record the required additional fields or accepted no-additional-fields applicability; implement any identified deltas in the shared audit workstream."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206537",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206537",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000116",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206537",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000116",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206537r960927_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206537r960927",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000116-DB-000057",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must use system clocks to generate time stamps for use in audit records and application data.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS that can use time stamp values obtained from or synchronized with the internal system clock used by the operating system.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to use time stamp values obtained from or synchronized with the internal system clock used by the operating system.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Using product documentation, verify that the DBMS uses current time stamp values obtained from or synchronized with the internal system clock used by the operating system. \n\nIf it is not able to, this is a finding.\n\nIf it is able to but is configured so that it does not do so, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Internal system clocks are typically a feature of server hardware and are maintained and used by the operating system. They are typically synchronized with an authoritative time server at regular intervals.\n\nWithout an internal system clock used as the reference for the time stored on each event to provide a trusted common reference for the time, forensic analysis would be impeded. Determining the correct time a particular event occurred on a system is critical when conducting forensic analysis and investigating system events. \n\nTime stamps generated by the internal system clock and used by the DBMS shall include both date and time. The time may be expressed in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), a modern continuation of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT), or local time with an offset from UTC.\n\nIf time sources other than the system time are used for audit records, the timeline of events can get skewed. This makes forensic analysis of the logs much less reliable.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42728",
            "V-32391"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000159"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "95927d52-6ace-52cb-8e71-e4ed16ed8b2a",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL server logging and timestamptz application audit fields use system time with subsecond precision; containers inherit MPN node time, and Vector preserves/labels the records.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-permission-table] app-permission-table\nAppend-only role-grant/revocation table includes actor user, subject user, tenant, role, action and timestamp; it does not record every request or failed authorization.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL server logging and timestamptz application audit fields use system time with subsecond precision; containers inherit MPN node time, and Vector preserves/labels the records.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206538",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206538",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000118",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206538",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000118",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206538r960930_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206538r960930",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000118-DB-000059",
          "rule_title": "The audit information produced by the DBMS must be protected from unauthorized read access.",
          "fix_text": "Apply controls and modify permissions to protect database audit log data from unauthorized access, whether stored in the database itself or at the OS level.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review locations of audit logs, both internal to the database and database audit logs located at the operating system level.\n\nVerify there are appropriate controls and permissions to protect the audit information from unauthorized access.\n\nIf appropriate controls and permissions do not exist, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If audit data were to become compromised, then competent forensic analysis and discovery of the true source of potentially malicious system activity is difficult, if not impossible, to achieve. In addition, access to audit records provides information an attacker could potentially use to his or her advantage.\n\nTo ensure the veracity of audit data, the information system and/or the application must protect audit information from any and all unauthorized access. This includes read, write, copy, etc.\n\nThis requirement can be achieved through multiple methods which will depend upon system architecture and design. Some commonly employed methods include ensuring log files enjoy the proper file system permissions utilizing file system protections and limiting log data location. \n\nAdditionally, applications with user interfaces to audit records should not allow for the unfettered manipulation of or access to those records via the application. If the application provides access to the audit data, the application becomes accountable for ensuring that audit information is protected from unauthorized access.\n\nAudit information includes all information (e.g., audit records, audit settings, and audit reports) needed to successfully audit information system activity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42730",
            "V-32393"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000162"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "8ff42d0f-1f2a-5dce-8a23-e11618cb89b7",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206539",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206539",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000119",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206539",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000119",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206539r960933_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206539r960933",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000119-DB-000060",
          "rule_title": "The audit information produced by the DBMS must be protected from unauthorized modification.",
          "fix_text": "Apply controls and modify permissions to protect database audit log data from unauthorized modification, whether stored in the database itself or at the OS level.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review locations of audit logs, both internal to the database and database audit logs located at the operating system level.\n\nVerify there are appropriate controls and permissions to protect the audit information from unauthorized modification.\n\nIf appropriate controls and permissions do not exist, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If audit data were to become compromised, then competent forensic analysis and discovery of the true source of potentially malicious system activity is impossible to achieve. \n\nTo ensure the veracity of audit data the information system and/or the application must protect audit information from unauthorized modification. \n\nThis requirement can be achieved through multiple methods that will depend upon system architecture and design. Some commonly employed methods include ensuring log files enjoy the proper file system permissions and limiting log data locations. \n\nApplications providing a user interface to audit data will leverage user permissions and roles identifying the user accessing the data and the corresponding rights that the user enjoys in order to make access decisions regarding the modification of audit data.\n\nAudit information includes all information (e.g., audit records, audit settings, and audit reports) needed to successfully audit information system activity. \n\nModification of database audit data could mask the theft of, or the unauthorized modification of, sensitive data stored in the database.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42731",
            "V-32394"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000163"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "54365d9e-c496-5c2f-87a0-81a1fc04745b",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206540",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206540",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000120",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206540",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000120",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206540r960936_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206540r960936",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000120-DB-000061",
          "rule_title": "The audit information produced by the DBMS must be protected from unauthorized deletion.",
          "fix_text": "Apply controls and modify permissions to protect database audit log data from unauthorized deletion, whether stored in the database itself or at the OS level.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review locations of audit logs, both internal to the database, and database audit logs located at the operating system level.\n\nVerify there are appropriate controls and permissions to protect the audit information from unauthorized deletion.\n\nIf appropriate controls and permissions do not exist, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If audit data were to become compromised, then competent forensic analysis and discovery of the true source of potentially malicious system activity is impossible to achieve.\n\nTo ensure the veracity of audit data, the information system and/or the application must protect audit information from unauthorized deletion. This requirement can be achieved through multiple methods which will depend upon system architecture and design.\n\nSome commonly employed methods include: ensuring log files enjoy the proper file system permissions utilizing file system protections; restricting access; and backing up log data to ensure log data is retained.\n\nApplications providing a user interface to audit data will leverage user permissions and roles identifying the user accessing the data and the corresponding rights the user enjoys in order make access decisions regarding the deletion of audit data.\n\nAudit information includes all information (e.g., audit records, audit settings, and audit reports) needed to successfully audit information system activity.\n\nDeletion of database audit data could mask the theft of, or the unauthorized modification of, sensitive data stored in the database.",
          "false_positives": "",
