          "uuid": "c7f25b5b-9ba3-50cb-ad23-21d94625e805",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206541",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206541",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000121",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206541",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000121",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206541r960939_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206541r960939",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000121-DB-000202",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must protect its audit features from unauthorized access.",
          "fix_text": "Apply or modify access controls and permissions (both within the DBMS and in the file system/operating system) to tools used to view or modify audit log data. Tools must be accessible by authorized personnel only.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the access permissions to tools used to view or modify audit log data. These tools may include features within the DBMS itself or software external to the database.\n\nIf appropriate permissions and access controls to prevent unauthorized access are not applied to these tools, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Protecting audit data also includes identifying and protecting the tools used to view and manipulate log data. \n\nDepending upon the log format and application, system and application log tools may provide the only means to manipulate and manage application and system log data. It is, therefore, imperative that access to audit tools be controlled and protected from unauthorized access. \n\nApplications providing tools to interface with audit data will leverage user permissions and roles identifying the user accessing the tools and the corresponding rights the user enjoys in order make access decisions regarding the access to audit tools.\n\nAudit tools include, but are not limited to, OS-provided audit tools, vendor-provided audit tools, and open source audit tools needed to successfully view and manipulate audit information system activity and records. \n\nIf an attacker were to gain access to audit tools, he could analyze audit logs for system weaknesses or weaknesses in the auditing itself. An attacker could also manipulate logs to hide evidence of malicious activity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42734",
            "V-32397"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001493"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "d5615a69-a1c4-56ef-84ac-53b572751884",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. DB runtime runs non-root on a dedicated PGDATA PVC; audit settings are administrative PostgreSQL settings and ordinary application DML users cannot edit server config. Offloaded logs are isolated behind Loki/Grafana service/group controls. Review the separate bundled-Keycloak admin-credential gap as a privileged service residual.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
