            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "When dealing with change control issues, it should be noted any changes to the hardware, software, and/or firmware components of the information system and/or application can have significant effects on the overall security of the system. \n\nIf the system were to allow any user to make changes to software libraries, then those changes might be implemented without undergoing the appropriate testing and approvals that are part of a robust change management process.\n\nAccordingly, only qualified and authorized individuals shall be allowed access to information system components for purposes of initiating changes, including upgrades and modifications.\n\nDBA and other privileged administrative or application owner accounts are granted privileges that allow actions that can have a great impact on database security and operation. It is especially important to grant privileged access to only those persons who are qualified and authorized to use them.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42751",
            "V-32414"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001499"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "f538d153-08f5-57aa-84c1-cf0361478679",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: DB installation/migration uses a separate administrative Secret and restricted job rather than application-user installer access; UDS/Kubernetes administration controls deployment changes. Document actual authorized administrators separately.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-identity] app-db-identity\nSeparate admin, horizon_app and openfga_app credentials exist. Bundled Keycloak is configured to connect using the admin DB role, a least-privilege candidate gap.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/manifests/secrets.yaml\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. DB installation/migration uses a separate administrative Secret and restricted job rather than application-user installer access; UDS/Kubernetes administration controls deployment changes. Document actual authorized administrators separately.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206546",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206546",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000133",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206546",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000133",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206546r960960_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206546r960960",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000133-DB-000199",
          "rule_title": "Database software, including DBMS configuration files, must be stored in dedicated directories, or DASD pools, separate from the host OS and other applications.",
          "fix_text": "Install all applications on directories separate from the DBMS software library directory. Relocate any directories or reinstall other application software that currently shares the DBMS software library directory.\n\nFor mainframe-based databases, locate database software and configuration files in separate DASD pools from other mainframe applications.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the DBMS software library directory and note other root directories located on the same disk directory or any subdirectories.\n\nIf any non-DBMS software directories exist on the disk directory, examine or investigate their use. If any of the directories are used by other applications, including third-party applications that use the DBMS, this is a finding.\n\nOnly applications that are required for the functioning and administration, not use, of the DBMS should be located in the same disk directory as the DBMS software libraries.\n\nIf other applications are located in the same directory as the DBMS, this is a finding.\n\nFor databases located on mainframes, confirm that the database and its configuration files are isolated in their own DASD pools.\n\nIf database software and database configuration files share DASD with other applications, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "When dealing with change control issues, it should be noted any changes to the hardware, software, and/or firmware components of the information system and/or application can potentially have significant effects on the overall security of the system.\n\nMultiple applications can provide a cumulative negative effect. A vulnerability and subsequent exploit to one application can lead to an exploit of other applications sharing the same security context. For example, an exploit to a web server process that leads to unauthorized administrative access to host system directories can most likely lead to a compromise of all applications hosted by the same system. Database software not installed using dedicated directories both threatens and is threatened by other hosted applications. Access controls defined for one application may by default provide access to the other application's database objects or directories. Any method that provides any level of separation of security context assists in the protection between applications.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42750",
            "V-32413"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001499"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "927334b9-8307-5608-9ace-847dba67f647",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_a_finding",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Compliant\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The database is in a dedicated container and PGDATA is a dedicated mounted persistent volume, separate from the node OS and other application containers. This is a source/configuration recommendation for the candidate filesystem layout.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "The database is in a dedicated container and PGDATA is a dedicated mounted persistent volume, separate from the node OS and other application containers. This is a source/configuration recommendation for the candidate filesystem layout."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206547",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206547",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000133",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206547",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000133",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206547r960960_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206547r960960",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000133-DB-000200",
          "rule_title": "Database objects (including but not limited to tables, indexes, storage, stored procedures, functions, triggers, links to software external to the DBMS, etc.) must be owned by database/DBMS principals authorized for ownership.",
          "fix_text": "Assign ownership of authorized objects to authorized object owner accounts.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system documentation to identify accounts authorized to own database objects. Review accounts that own objects in the database(s).\n\nIf any database objects are found to be owned by users not authorized to own database objects, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Within the database, object ownership implies full privileges to the owned object, including the privilege to assign access to the owned objects to other subjects. Database functions and procedures can be coded using definer's rights. This allows anyone who utilizes the object to perform the actions if they were the owner. If not properly managed, this can lead to privileged actions being taken by unauthorized individuals.\n\nConversely, if critical tables or other objects rely on unauthorized owner accounts, these objects may be lost when an account is removed.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42749",
            "V-32412"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001499"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "0061cb5b-c033-5ae1-ab13-252bc68432c3",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206548",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206548",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000133",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206548",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000133",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206548r960960_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206548r960960",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000133-DB-000362",
          "rule_title": "The role(s)/group(s) used to modify database structure (including but not necessarily limited to tables, indexes, storage, etc.) and logic modules (stored procedures, functions, triggers, links to software external to the DBMS, etc.) must be restricted to authorized users.",
          "fix_text": "Revoke unauthorized memberships in the DBMS modification group(s)/role(s).",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Identify the group(s)/role(s) established for DBMS modification.\n\nObtain the list of users in those group(s)/roles.\n\nIdentify the individuals authorized to modify the DBMS.\n\nIf unauthorized access to the group(s)/role(s) has been granted, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If the DBMS were to allow any user to make changes to database structure or logic, then those changes might be implemented without undergoing the appropriate testing and approvals that are part of a robust change management process.\n\nAccordingly, only qualified and authorized individuals shall be allowed to obtain access to information system components for purposes of initiating changes, including upgrades and modifications.\n\nUnmanaged changes that occur to the database software libraries or configuration can lead to unauthorized or compromised installations.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
