        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206549",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206549",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000141",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206549",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000141",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206549r960963_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206549r960963",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000141-DB-000090",
          "rule_title": "Default demonstration and sample databases, database objects, and applications must be removed.",
          "fix_text": "Remove any demonstration and sample databases, database applications, objects, and files from the DBMS.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review vendor documentation and vendor websites to identify vendor-provided demonstration or sample databases, database applications, objects, and files. \n\nReview the DBMS to determine if any of the demonstration and sample databases, database applications, or files are installed in the database or are included with the DBMS application.\n\nIf any are present in the database or are included with the DBMS application, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information systems are capable of providing a wide variety of functions and services. Some of the functions and services, provided by default, may not be necessary to support essential organizational operations (e.g., key missions, functions).\n\nIt is detrimental for software products to provide, or install by default, functionality exceeding requirements or mission objectives. Examples include, but are not limited to, installing advertising software, demonstrations, or browser plugins not related to requirements or providing a wide array of functionality, not required for every mission, that cannot be disabled.\n\nDBMSs must adhere to the principles of least functionality by providing only essential capabilities.\n\nDemonstration and sample database objects and applications present publicly known attack points for malicious users. These demonstration and sample objects are meant to provide simple examples of coding specific functions and are not developed to prevent vulnerabilities from being introduced to the DBMS and host system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42760",
            "V-32423"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000381"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "cc8ce4d3-bafa-5fb1-b28c-a86b8f92e080",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The custom Postgres runtime is assembled for the required PostGIS/pgvector/pgaudit functions and database migrations create the application/identity stores; the image does not ship a database sample application. Retain a final extension/database inventory to identify image-provided extras.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-extensions] app-db-extensions\nMigration installs pgvector/PostGIS and attempts pgaudit, catching pgaudit failures as warnings; installation and effective audit classes must not be assumed from preload.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The custom Postgres runtime is assembled for the required PostGIS/pgvector/pgaudit functions and database migrations create the application/identity stores; the image does not ship a database sample application. Retain a final extension/database inventory to identify image-provided extras.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
