          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The custom Postgres runtime is assembled for the required PostGIS/pgvector/pgaudit functions and database migrations create the application/identity stores; the image does not ship a database sample application. Retain a final extension/database inventory to identify image-provided extras.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-extensions] app-db-extensions\nMigration installs pgvector/PostGIS and attempts pgaudit, catching pgaudit failures as warnings; installation and effective audit classes must not be assumed from preload.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The custom Postgres runtime is assembled for the required PostGIS/pgvector/pgaudit functions and database migrations create the application/identity stores; the image does not ship a database sample application. Retain a final extension/database inventory to identify image-provided extras.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206551",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206551",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000141",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206551",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000141",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206551r960963_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206551r960963",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000141-DB-000092",
          "rule_title": "Unused database components that are integrated in the DBMS and cannot be uninstalled must be disabled.",
          "fix_text": "Disable any unused components or features that cannot be uninstalled.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the DBMS for unused components of the system that cannot be uninstalled.\n\nIf unused components or features are present on the system, can be disabled, and are not disabled, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information systems are capable of providing a wide variety of functions and services. Some of the functions and services, provided by default, may not be necessary to support essential organizational operations (e.g., key missions, functions). \n\nIt is detrimental for software products to provide, or install by default, functionality exceeding requirements or mission objectives. \n\nDBMSs must adhere to the principles of least functionality by providing only essential capabilities.\n\nUnused, unnecessary DBMS components increase the attack vector for the DBMS by introducing additional targets for attack. By minimizing the services and applications installed on the system, the number of potential vulnerabilities is reduced. Components of the system that are unused and cannot be uninstalled must be disabled. The techniques available for disabling components will vary by DBMS product, OS, and the nature of the component and may include DBMS configuration settings, OS service settings, OS file access security, and DBMS user/role permissions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42763",
            "V-32426"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000381"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "4f67087f-9fd7-5c1c-9f76-2bf69c88f865",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The custom Postgres runtime is assembled for the required PostGIS/pgvector/pgaudit functions and database migrations create the application/identity stores; the image does not ship a database sample application. Retain a final extension/database inventory to identify image-provided extras.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-extensions] app-db-extensions\nMigration installs pgvector/PostGIS and attempts pgaudit, catching pgaudit failures as warnings; installation and effective audit classes must not be assumed from preload.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The custom Postgres runtime is assembled for the required PostGIS/pgvector/pgaudit functions and database migrations create the application/identity stores; the image does not ship a database sample application. Retain a final extension/database inventory to identify image-provided extras.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206552",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206552",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000141",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206552",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000141",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206552r960963_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206552r960963",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000141-DB-000093",
          "rule_title": "Access to external executables must be disabled or restricted.",
          "fix_text": "Disable use of or remove any external application executable object definitions that are not authorized.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the database for definitions of application executable objects stored external to the database.\n\nDetermine if there are methods to disable use or access, or to remove definitions for external executable objects.\n\nVerify each application executable object listed is authorized by the ISSO. If any are not, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information systems are capable of providing a wide variety of functions and services. Some of the functions and services, provided by default, may not be necessary to support essential organizational operations (e.g., key missions, functions). \n\nIt is detrimental for applications to provide, or install by default, functionality exceeding requirements or mission objectives. \n\nApplications must adhere to the principles of least functionality by providing only essential capabilities.\n\nDBMSs may spawn additional external processes to execute procedures that are defined in the DBMS but stored in external host files (external procedures). The spawned process used to execute the external procedure may operate within a different OS security context than the DBMS and provide unauthorized access to the host system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42764",
            "V-32427"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000381"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "1aa09621-b9cd-59f6-8614-3a8f247dcda1",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206553",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206553",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000142",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206553",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000142",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206553r1043177_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206553r1043177",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000142-DB-000094",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must be configured to prohibit or restrict the use of organization-defined functions, ports, protocols, and/or services, as defined in the PPSM CAL and vulnerability assessments.",
          "fix_text": "Disable functions, ports, protocols, and services that are not approved.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the DBMS settings and local documentation for functions, ports, protocols, and services that are not approved. If any are found, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In order to prevent unauthorized connection of devices, unauthorized transfer of information, or unauthorized tunneling (i.e., embedding of data types within data types), organizations must disable or restrict unused or unnecessary physical and logical ports/protocols/services on information systems.\n\nApplications are capable of providing a wide variety of functions and services. Some of the functions and services provided by default may not be necessary to support essential organizational operations. Additionally, it is sometimes convenient to provide multiple services from a single component (e.g., email and web services); however, doing so increases risk over limiting the services provided by any one component. \n\nTo support the requirements and principles of least functionality, the application must support the organizational requirements providing only essential capabilities and limiting the use of ports, protocols, and/or services to only those required, authorized, and approved to conduct official business or to address authorized quality of life issues.\n\nDatabase Management Systems using ports, protocols, and services deemed unsafe are open to attack through those ports, protocols, and services. This can allow unauthorized access to the database and through the database to other components of the information system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42765",
            "V-32428"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000382"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "cf3d1856-9fb0-5a25-a349-e1af1649663e",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL has an internal ClusterIP service with declared application port5432 and no candidate public DB ingress; mesh/default-deny govern connectivity. Final approved PPSM/profile mapping remains required.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL has an internal ClusterIP service with declared application port5432 and no candidate public DB ingress; mesh/default-deny govern connectivity. Final approved PPSM/profile mapping remains required.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206554",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206554",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000148",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206554",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000148",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206554r1051115_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206554r1051115",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000148-DB-000103",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must uniquely identify and authenticate organizational users (or processes acting on behalf of organizational users).",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS settings to uniquely identify and authenticate all organizational users who log on/connect to the system.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS settings to determine whether organizational users are uniquely identified and authenticated when logging on/connecting to the system.\n\nIf organizational users are not uniquely identified and authenticated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "To assure accountability and prevent unauthenticated access, organizational users must be identified and authenticated to prevent potential misuse and compromise of the system. \n\nOrganizational users include organizational employees or individuals the organization deems to have equivalent status of employees (e.g., contractors). Organizational users (and any processes acting on behalf of users) must be uniquely identified and authenticated for all accesses, except the following:\n\n(i) Accesses explicitly identified and documented by the organization. Organizations document specific user actions that can be performed on the information system without identification or authentication; and \n(ii) Accesses that occur through authorized use of group authenticators without individual authentication. Organizations may require unique identification of individuals using shared accounts, for detailed accountability of individual activity.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42779",
            "V-32442"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000764"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "8cc59908-d9f1-54ee-af74-fc02a242ad13",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Separate application/admin/service credentials and native PostgreSQL role login identify processes; human application users are identified by OIDC and actor-aware domain audit. Non-organizational direct DB access is not enabled as an anonymous path.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-identity] app-db-identity\nSeparate admin, horizon_app and openfga_app credentials exist. Bundled Keycloak is configured to connect using the admin DB role, a least-privilege candidate gap.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/manifests/secrets.yaml\n\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-permission-table] app-permission-table\nAppend-only role-grant/revocation table includes actor user, subject user, tenant, role, action and timestamp; it does not record every request or failed authorization.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Separate application/admin/service credentials and native PostgreSQL role login identify processes; human application users are identified by OIDC and actor-aware domain audit. Non-organizational direct DB access is not enabled as an anonymous path.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206555",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206555",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000164",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206555",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000164",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206555r1268160_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206555r1268160",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000164-DB-000401",
          "rule_title": "If DBMS authentication, using passwords, is employed, the DBMS must enforce the DoW standards for password complexity and lifetime.",
          "fix_text": "If the use of passwords is not needed, configure the DBMS to prevent their use if it is capable of this; if it is not, institute policies and procedures to prohibit their use.\n\nIf the DBMS can inherit password complexity rules from the operating system or access control program, configure it to do so.\n\nOtherwise, use DBMS configuration parameters and/or custom code to enforce the following rules for passwords:\na. Minimum of 15 characters, including at least one of each of the following character sets:\n- Uppercase.\n- Lowercase.\n- Numerics.\n- Special characters (e.g., ~ ! @ # $ % ^ & * ( ) _ + = - ' [ ] / ? > <).\nb. Minimum number of characters changed from previous password: 50 percent of the minimum password length; that is, eight.\nc. Password lifetime limits for interactive accounts: Minimum 24 hours, maximum 180 days.\nd. Password lifetime limits for non-interactive accounts: Minimum 24 hours, maximum 365 days.\ne. Number of password changes before an old one may be reused: Minimum of five.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If DBMS authentication, using passwords, is not employed, this is not a finding.\n\nIf the DBMS is configured to inherit password complexity and lifetime rules from the operating system or access control program, this is not a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS settings relating to password complexity. Determine whether the following rules are enforced. If any are not, this is a finding.\na. minimum of 15 characters, including at least one of each of the following character sets:\n- Uppercase.\n- Lowercase.\n- Numerics.\n- Special characters (e.g., ~ ! @ # $ % ^ & * ( ) _ + = - ' [ ] / ? > <).\nb. Minimum number of characters changed from previous password: 50 percent of the minimum password length; that is, eight.\n\nReview the DBMS settings relating to password lifetime. Determine whether the following rules are enforced. If any are not, this is a finding.\na. Password lifetime limits for interactive accounts: Minimum 24 hours, maximum 180 days.\nb. Password lifetime limits for noninteractive accounts: Minimum 24 hours, maximum 365 days.\nc. Number of password changes before an old one may be reused: Minimum of five.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "OS/enterprise authentication and identification must be used (SRG-APP-000023-DB-000001). Native DBMS authentication may be used only when circumstances make it unavoidable; and must be documented and authorizing official (AO) approved.\n\nThe DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates. Authentication based on user ID and password may be used only when it is not possible to employ a PKI certificate, and requires AO approval.\n\nIn such cases, the DoW standards for password complexity and lifetime must be implemented. DBMS products that can inherit the rules for these from the operating system or access control program (e.g., Microsoft Active Directory) must be configured to do so. For other DBMSs, the rules must be enforced using available configuration parameters or custom code.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-75897",
            "V-61407"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004066"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "d04b348a-4786-5a24-acfa-4b5f719d6b05",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Actual SQL credentials are noninteractive deployment identities generated with192 bits of entropy, rather than human-selected passwords. Shared UDS handles human password/MFA policy. This provides a defensible machine-account alternative to literal character-class/history checks, but the official text includes noninteractive lifetimes, so accepted machine-account generation/rotation tailoring must be attached rather than claiming unconditional compliance.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Document approved machine-account password generation/rotation policy; if tailoring is rejected, implement the exact native password constraints and lifetime controls.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-machine-secrets] re-database-machine-secrets\nService/admin DB passwords generated with openssl rand -hex24 when not supplied, retained from existing Secrets on upgrade; app/openfga/admin are explicit deployment identities, not per-human accounts.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Actual SQL credentials are noninteractive deployment identities generated with192 bits of entropy, rather than human-selected passwords. Shared UDS handles human password/MFA policy. This provides a defensible machine-account alternative to literal character-class/history checks, but the official text includes noninteractive lifetimes, so accepted machine-account generation/rotation tailoring must be attached rather than claiming unconditional compliance.\n\nNext action: Document approved machine-account password generation/rotation policy; if tailoring is rejected, implement the exact native password constraints and lifetime controls."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206556",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206556",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000171",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206556",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000171",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206556r1268164_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206556r1268164",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000171-DB-000074",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must for password-based authentication, store passwords using an approved salted key derivation function, preferably using a keyed hash.",
          "fix_text": "Develop, document, and maintain a list of DBMS database objects, database configuration files, associated scripts, applications defined within or external to the DBMS that access the database, and DBMS/user environment files/settings in the System Security Plan.\n\nRecord whether they do or do not contain DBMS passwords. If passwords are present, ensure they are correctly hashed using one-way, salted hashing functions, and that the hashes are protected by host system security.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the list of DBMS database objects, database configuration files, associated scripts, and applications defined within and external to the DBMS that access the database. The list should also include files or settings used to configure the operational environment for the DBMS and for interactive DBMS user accounts.\n\nDetermine whether any DBMS database objects, database configuration files, associated scripts, applications defined within or external to the DBMS that access the database, and DBMS/user environment files/settings contain database passwords. If any do, confirm that DBMS passwords stored internally or externally to the DBMS are hashed using FIPS-approved cryptographic algorithms and include a salt. If any passwords are stored in clear text, this is a finding. If any passwords are stored with reversible encryption, this is a finding.  If any passwords are stored using unsalted hashes, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates.\n\nAuthentication based on user ID and password may be used only when it is not possible to employ a PKI certificate, and requires authorizing official (AO) approval.\n\nIn such cases, database passwords stored in clear text, using reversible encryption, or using unsalted hashes would be vulnerable to unauthorized disclosure. Database passwords must always be in the form of one-way, salted hashes when stored internally or externally to the DBMS.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42805",
            "V-32468"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004062"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "24cb3e91-58d3-5f5d-ba96-84a33a9d6342",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The candidate uses PostgreSQL18 and CREATE/ALTER ROLE passwords without overriding password_encryption; upstream default stores SCRAM-SHA-256 representations. Inspect existing PGDATA hashes/settings before claiming the same for upgraded volumes.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-password] pg-password\nPostgreSQL18 defaults password_encryption to SCRAM-SHA-256. Existing persistent data/configuration can override defaults; generated new credentials and selected image must be distinguished from existing volumes.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-connection.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The candidate uses PostgreSQL18 and CREATE/ALTER ROLE passwords without overriding password_encryption; upstream default stores SCRAM-SHA-256 representations. Inspect existing PGDATA hashes/settings before claiming the same for upgraded volumes.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206557",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206557",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000172",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206557",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000172",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206557r1268165_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206557r1268165",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000172-DB-000075",
          "rule_title": "If passwords are used for authentication, the DBMS must transmit only encrypted representations of passwords.",
          "fix_text": "Configure encryption for transmission of passwords across the network. If the database does not provide encryption for logon events natively, employ encryption at the OS or network level.\n\nEnsure passwords remain encrypted from source to destination.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review configuration settings for encrypting passwords in transit across the network. If passwords are not encrypted, this is a finding. \n\nIf it is determined that passwords are passed unencrypted at any point along the transmission path between the source and destination, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved PKI certificates.\n\nAuthentication based on User ID and Password may be used only when it is not possible to employ a PKI certificate, and requires authorizing official (AO) approval.\n\nIn such cases, passwords need to be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords during transmission.\n\nDBMS passwords sent in clear text format across the network are vulnerable to discovery by unauthorized users. Disclosure of passwords may easily lead to unauthorized access to the database.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42806",
            "V-32469"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000197"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "32652ab4-b94a-5a71-8a1b-a34f97f97b38",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206558",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206558",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000175",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206558",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000175",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206558r1268166_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206558r1268166",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000175-DB-000067",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS, when utilizing Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)-based authentication, must validate certificates by performing RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to validate certificates by performing RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS configuration to verify that certificates being accepted by the DBMS are validated by performing RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation.\n\nIf certificates are not being validated by performing RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved PKI certificates.\n\nA certificate’s certification path is the path from the end entity certificate to a trusted root certification authority (CA). Certification path validation is necessary for a relying party to make an informed decision regarding acceptance of an end entity certificate. Certification path validation includes checks such as certificate issuer trust, time validity and revocation status for each certificate in the certification path. Revocation status information for CA and subject certificates in a certification path is commonly provided via certificate revocation lists (CRLs) or online certificate status protocol (OCSP) responses.\n\nDatabase Management Systems that do not validate certificates by performing RFC 5280-compliant certification path validation are in danger of accepting certificates that are invalid and/or counterfeit. This could allow unauthorized access to the database.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42812",
            "V-32475"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "733ec5b4-c6e5-5902-8fd2-7716be3ca3a9",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Selected PostgreSQL uses native password/service roles, not certificate-to-DB-user authentication. Mesh workload certificates protect transport and are not PostgreSQL PKI user mapping. Document the official PKI-based-authentication applicability boundary rather than configuring an unused DB certificate login.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain approved password-authentication/mesh architecture and rule-specific applicability; reassess if native DB PKI is introduced.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-identity] app-db-identity\nSeparate admin, horizon_app and openfga_app credentials exist. Bundled Keycloak is configured to connect using the admin DB role, a least-privilege candidate gap.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/manifests/secrets.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Selected PostgreSQL uses native password/service roles, not certificate-to-DB-user authentication. Mesh workload certificates protect transport and are not PostgreSQL PKI user mapping. Document the official PKI-based-authentication applicability boundary rather than configuring an unused DB certificate login.\n\nNext action: Retain approved password-authentication/mesh architecture and rule-specific applicability; reassess if native DB PKI is introduced."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206559",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206559",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000176",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206559",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000176",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206559r1268167_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206559r1268167",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000176-DB-000068",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must enforce authorized access to all Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) private keys stored/utilized by the DBMS.",
          "fix_text": "Store all DBMS PKI private keys in a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic module. Ensure access to the DBMS PKI private keys is restricted to only authenticated and authorized users.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS configuration to determine whether appropriate access controls exist to protect the DBMS's private key(s). If the DMBS’s private key(s) are not stored in a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic module, this is a finding.\n\nIf access to the DBMS’s private key(s) is not restricted to authenticated and authorized users, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved PKI certificates. PKI certificate-based authentication is performed by requiring the certificate holder to cryptographically prove possession of the corresponding private key.\n\nIf the private key is stolen, an attacker can use the private key(s) to impersonate the certificate holder. In cases where the DBMS-stored private keys are used to authenticate the DBMS to the system’s clients, loss of the corresponding private keys would allow an attacker to successfully perform undetected man in the middle attacks against the DBMS system and its clients.\n\nBoth the holder of a digital certificate and the issuing authority must take careful measures to protect the corresponding private key. Private keys should always be generated and protected in FIPS 140-2- or 140-3- validated cryptographic modules.\n\nAll access to the private key(s) of the DBMS must be restricted to authorized and authenticated users. If unauthorized users have access to one or more of the DBMS's private keys, an attacker could gain access to the key(s) and use them to impersonate the database on the network or otherwise perform unauthorized actions.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42813",
            "V-32476"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000186"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "bb9ed80e-6636-5596-a62c-f99a73aab9de",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Mesh/gateway private keys are managed in the platform Secret/identity boundary rather than world-readable database files; PostgreSQL native client private keys are not configured in the selected password-auth candidate.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[core-gateway] core-gateway\nGateway redirects HTTP to HTTPS and requires TLS 1.3 by default, optionally TLS 1.2; credentialName references Kubernetes TLS secrets.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/charts/uds-istio-config/templates/gateway.yaml\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Mesh/gateway private keys are managed in the platform Secret/identity boundary rather than world-readable database files; PostgreSQL native client private keys are not configured in the selected password-auth candidate.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206560",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206560",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000177",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206560",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000177",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206560r1268168_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206560r1268168",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000177-DB-000069",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must map the Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)-authenticated identity to an associated user account.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to map the authenticated identity directly to the DBMS user account.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS configuration to verify DBMS user accounts are being mapped directly to unique identifying information within the validated PKI certificate.\n\nIf user accounts are not being mapped to authenticated identities, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved PKI certificates. Once a PKI certificate has been validated, it must be mapped to a DBMS user account for the authenticated identity to be meaningful to the DBMS and useful for authorization decisions.",
          "false_positives": "",
