        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206561",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206561",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000178",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206561",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000178",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206561r1268169_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206561r1268169",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000178-DB-000083",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must obscure feedback of authentication information during the authentication process to protect the information from possible exploitation/use by unauthorized individuals.",
          "fix_text": "Modify and configure each non-compliant application, tool, or feature associated with the DBMS/database so that it does not display authentication secrets.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If all interaction with the user for purposes of authentication is handled by a software component separate from the DBMS, this is not a finding.\n\nIf any application, tool or feature associated with the DBMS/database displays any authentication secrets (to include PINs and passwords) during - or after - the authentication process, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication is DoW-approved PKI certificates.\n\nNormally, with PKI authentication, the interaction with the user for authentication will be handled by a software component separate from the DBMS, such as ActivIdentity ActivClient. However, in cases where the DBMS controls the interaction, this requirement applies.\n\nTo prevent the compromise of authentication information such as passwords and PINs during the authentication process, the feedback from the system must not provide any information that would allow an unauthorized user to compromise the authentication mechanism. \n\nObfuscation of user-provided authentication secrets when typed into the system is a method used in addressing this risk. \n\nDisplaying asterisks when a user types in a password or a smart card PIN is an example of obscuring feedback of authentication secrets.\n\nThis calls for review of applications, which will require collaboration with the application developers. It is recognized that in many cases, the database administrator (DBA) is organizationally separate from the application developers, and may have limited, if any, access to source code. Nevertheless, protections of this type are so important to the secure operation of databases that they must not be ignored. At a minimum, the DBA must attempt to obtain assurances from the development organization that this issue has been addressed, and must document what has been discovered.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42816",
            "V-32479"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000206"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "d85e840b-1a23-5a13-8d53-f543fa72913b",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Native PostgreSQL password authentication does not echo a submitted password to the user, and the application log serializer redacts credentials. Avoid turning verbose SQL/audit parameter logging on without preserving that protection.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-password] pg-password\nPostgreSQL18 defaults password_encryption to SCRAM-SHA-256. Existing persistent data/configuration can override defaults; generated new credentials and selected image must be distinguished from existing volumes.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-connection.html\n\n[logging] API logging\nCurrent HTTP logger includes service, request/trace IDs, event, remote address and response status, with secret redaction. Its common bindings do not add authenticated actor identity; other records/correlation must be checked.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/core/observability/logging.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Native PostgreSQL password authentication does not echo a submitted password to the user, and the application log serializer redacts credentials. Avoid turning verbose SQL/audit parameter logging on without preserving that protection.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206562",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206562",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000179",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206562",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000179",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206562r961050_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206562r961050",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000179-DB-000114",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must use NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules for cryptographic operations.",
          "fix_text": "Utilize NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules for all cryptographic operations.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS configuration to verify it is using NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules for cryptographic operations.\n\nIf NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated modules are not being used for all cryptographic operations, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Use of weak or not validated cryptographic algorithms undermines the purposes of utilizing encryption and digital signatures to protect data. Weak algorithms can be easily broken and not validated cryptographic modules may not implement algorithms correctly. Unapproved cryptographic modules or algorithms should not be relied on for authentication, confidentiality, or integrity. Weak cryptography could allow an attacker to gain access to and modify data stored in the database as well as the administration settings of the DBMS.\n\nApplications (including DBMSs) utilizing cryptography are required to use approved NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules that meet the requirements of applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, standards, and guidance. \n\nNSA Type-X (where X=1, 2, 3, 4) products are NSA-certified, hardware-based encryption modules.\n\nThe standard for validating cryptographic modules will transition to the NIST FIPS 140-3 publication.\n\nFIPS 140-2 modules can remain active for up to five years after validation or until September 21, 2026, when the FIPS 140-2 validations will be moved to the historical list. Even on the historical list, CMVP supports the purchase and use of these modules for existing systems. While Federal Agencies decide when they move to FIPS 140-3 only modules, purchasers are reminded that for several years there may be a limited selection of FIPS 140-3 modules from which to choose. CMVP recommends purchasers consider all modules that appear on the Validated Modules Search Page:\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/validated-modules\n\nMore information on the FIPS 140-3 transition can be found here: \nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/fips-140-3-transition-effort/",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42817",
            "V-32480"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000803"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "fdc60f7a-5177-5bb1-93f1-6a08e67bcb4b",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Exact selected PostgreSQL image provenance and actual build SBOM identify PostgreSQL18.6-r7, CMVP5132 OpenSSL3.4.0-r5 and approved-only fipshardened configuration. Custom extension staging preserves runtime libcrypto/libssl instead of replacing them. This resolves DBMS cryptographic-module selection for its crypto/signature/hash/confidentiality operations; it does not assert that an unspecified PVC backend has encryption enabled or that the DBMS implements an application signing workflow.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the exact DB image/module certificate mapping; handle backend at-rest encryption selection and any unused signing-function applicability in the separate storage/component assessment.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-compose] re-images-compose\nActual selected source pins node-fips24.22.1-slim@b61e57c2..., nginx-fips1.31.8@72d1e862..., postgres-fips18.6@9760ae95..., python-fips3.13.18; release runtimeSource binds exact platform manifests and PostgreSQL source/build definition.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/compose.yml\n\n[re-images-pg-sbom] re-images-pg-sbom\nActual selected PostgreSQL build source9d3725b/run41699186459 SBOM contains postgresql18.6-r7, NIST-CMVP-5132, OpenSSL FIPS provider3.4.0-r5 and fipshardened configuration. The multistage extension copy deliberately excludes/reuses runtime libcrypto/libssl, preserving base module. Release verifies selected PostgreSQL definition/context hashes and exact platform digest3431665e....\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/41699186459/artifacts/13263507123\n\n[re-images-vendor] re-images-vendor\nVendor documents FIPS images configured in approved-only mode; using these images is concrete enabled configuration, not an unconfigured capability. FIPS apps do not require host kernel FIPS because validated user-space entropy is included.\nhttps://www.chainguard.dev/docs/platform/fips/faqs\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Exact selected PostgreSQL image provenance and actual build SBOM identify PostgreSQL18.6-r7, CMVP5132 OpenSSL3.4.0-r5 and approved-only fipshardened configuration. Custom extension staging preserves runtime libcrypto/libssl instead of replacing them. This resolves DBMS cryptographic-module selection for its crypto/signature/hash/confidentiality operations; it does not assert that an unspecified PVC backend has encryption enabled or that the DBMS implements an application signing workflow.\n\nNext action: Retain the exact DB image/module certificate mapping; handle backend at-rest encryption selection and any unused signing-function applicability in the separate storage/component assessment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206563",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206563",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000180",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206563",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000180",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206563r961053_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206563r961053",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000180-DB-000115",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must uniquely identify and authenticate non-organizational users (or processes acting on behalf of non-organizational users).",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS settings to uniquely identify and authenticate all non-organizational users who log onto the system.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS settings to determine whether non-organizational users are uniquely identified and authenticated when logging onto the system.\n\nIf non-organizational users are not uniquely identified and authenticated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Non-organizational users include all information system users other than organizational users, which include organizational employees or individuals the organization deems to have equivalent status of employees (e.g., contractors, guest researchers, individuals from allied nations). \n\nNon-organizational users shall be uniquely identified and authenticated for all accesses other than those accesses explicitly identified and documented by the organization when related to the use of anonymous access, such as accessing a web server. \n\nAccordingly, a risk assessment is used in determining the authentication needs of the organization. \n\nScalability, practicality, and security are simultaneously considered in balancing the need to ensure ease of use for access to federal information and information systems with the need to protect and adequately mitigate risk to organizational operations, organizational assets, individuals, other organizations, and the Nation.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42818",
            "V-32481"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000804"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "40629ee0-a68c-506f-9918-5bacd6d57e66",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Separate application/admin/service credentials and native PostgreSQL role login identify processes; human application users are identified by OIDC and actor-aware domain audit. Non-organizational direct DB access is not enabled as an anonymous path.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-identity] app-db-identity\nSeparate admin, horizon_app and openfga_app credentials exist. Bundled Keycloak is configured to connect using the admin DB role, a least-privilege candidate gap.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/manifests/secrets.yaml\n\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-permission-table] app-permission-table\nAppend-only role-grant/revocation table includes actor user, subject user, tenant, role, action and timestamp; it does not record every request or failed authorization.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Separate application/admin/service credentials and native PostgreSQL role login identify processes; human application users are identified by OIDC and actor-aware domain audit. Non-organizational direct DB access is not enabled as an anonymous path.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206564",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206564",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000211",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206564",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000211",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206564r1137655_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206564r1137655",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000211-DB-000122",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must separate user functionality (including user interface services) from database management functionality.",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS to separate database administration and general user functionality.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS settings and vendor documentation to verify that administrative functionality is separate from user functionality.\n\nIf administrator and general user functionality are not separated either physically or logically, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information system management functionality includes functions necessary to administer databases, network components, workstations, or servers and typically requires privileged user access. \n\nThe separation of user functionality from information system management functionality is either physical or logical and is accomplished by using different computers, different central processing units, different instances of the operating system, different network addresses, combinations of these methods, or other methods, as appropriate. \n\nAn example of this type of separation is observed in web administrative interfaces that use separate authentication methods for users of any other information system resources. \n\nThis may include isolating the administrative interface on a different domain and with additional access controls.\n\nIf administrative functionality or information regarding DBMS management is presented on an interface available for users, information on DBMS settings may be inadvertently made available to the user.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42851",
            "V-32514"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001082"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "7557a31e-1332-59fe-b7d6-de234a714ebf",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered package selects shared UDS identity and contains no bundled Keycloak database consumer. Dedicated app SQL role, separate database service and separate UDS identity/UI/administration implement logical separation.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain this source and delivered-release mapping with the component checklist; confirm applicability and accepted inheritance at sign-off.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-postgres-pod] re-database-postgres-pod\nDedicated PostgreSQL container/PVC and ClusterIP SQL service; PGDATA persists separately from read-only image. Native credentials from a managed Secret; generated initdb config in PGDATA. No human SQL login route is publicly exposed by Package.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-database-package-network] re-database-package-network\nUDS ambient mesh enrolls horizon namespace and controls SQL connectivity through namespace policy. Public exposes API/web/NATS and bundledKeycloak only when selected; no public PostgreSQL endpoint.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The delivered package selects shared UDS identity and contains no bundled Keycloak database consumer. Dedicated app SQL role, separate database service and separate UDS identity/UI/administration implement logical separation.\n\nNext action: Retain this source and delivered-release mapping with the component checklist; confirm applicability and accepted inheritance at sign-off."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206565",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206565",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000220",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206565",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000220",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206565r1043179_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206565r1043179",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000220-DB-000149",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must invalidate session identifiers upon user logout or other session termination.",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS settings to terminate sessions, invalidating their session identifiers, upon user logout.\n\nConfigure DBMS settings to terminate sessions, invalidating their session identifiers, upon the occurrence of any organization- or policy-defined session termination event.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS settings and vendor documentation to verify user sessions are terminated, and session identifiers invalidated, upon user logout. If they are not, this is a finding.\n\nReview system documentation and organization policy to identify other events that should result in session terminations.\n\nIf other session termination events are defined, review DBMS settings to verify occurrences of these events would cause session termination, invalidating the session identifiers.\n\nIf occurrences of defined session terminating events do not cause session terminations, invalidating the session identifiers, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Captured sessions can be reused in \"replay\" attacks. This requirement limits the ability of adversaries to capture and continue to employ previously valid session IDs.\n\nThis requirement focuses on communications protection for the DBMS session rather than for the network packet. The intent of this control is to establish grounds for confidence at each end of a communications session in the ongoing identity of the other party and in the validity of the information being transmitted. \n\nSession IDs are tokens generated by DBMSs to uniquely identify a user's (or process's) session. DBMSs will make access decisions and execute logic based on the session ID.\n\nUnique session IDs help to reduce predictability of said identifiers. Unique session IDs address man-in-the-middle attacks, including session hijacking or insertion of false information into a session. If the attacker is unable to identify or guess the session information related to pending application traffic, they will have more difficulty in hijacking the session or otherwise manipulating valid sessions. \n\nWhen a user logs out, or when any other session termination event occurs, the DBMS must terminate the user session(s) to minimize the potential for sessions to be hijacked.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42860",
            "V-32523"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001185"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "142f127c-1e96-559b-bf3f-2accccb84d64",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL server controls connection/backend session identifiers and terminates a connection/session on client disconnect; clients cannot preserve an authenticated DB session merely by replaying a browser-style session ID. The product provides native logout/connection close.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL server controls connection/backend session identifiers and terminates a connection/session on client disconnect; clients cannot preserve an authenticated DB session merely by replaying a browser-style session ID. The product provides native logout/connection close.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206566",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206566",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000223",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206566",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000223",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206566r1043180_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206566r1043180",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000223-DB-000168",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must recognize only system-generated session identifiers.",
          "fix_text": "Utilize a DBMS product that only recognizes session identifiers that are system-generated.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS settings and vendor documentation to determine whether the DBMS recognizes session identifiers that are not system-generated.\n\nIf the DBMS recognizes session identifiers that are not system generated, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "DBMSs utilize sessions and session identifiers to control application behavior and user access. If an attacker can guess the session identifier or can inject or manually insert session information, the session may be compromised.\n\nThis requirement focuses on communications protection for the DBMS session rather than for the network packet. The intent of this control is to establish grounds for confidence at each end of a communications session in the ongoing identity of the other party and in the validity of the information being transmitted. \n\nThe DBMS must recognize only system-generated session identifiers. If an attacker were able to generate a session with a non-system-generated session identifier and have it recognized by the system, the attacker could gain access to the system without passing through access controls designed to limit database sessions to authorized users.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42863",
            "V-32526"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001664"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "f901c4ed-6b8f-5c02-a489-0fafb2b373f6",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL server controls connection/backend session identifiers and terminates a connection/session on client disconnect; clients cannot preserve an authenticated DB session merely by replaying a browser-style session ID. The product provides native logout/connection close.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL server controls connection/backend session identifiers and terminates a connection/session on client disconnect; clients cannot preserve an authenticated DB session merely by replaying a browser-style session ID. The product provides native logout/connection close.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206567",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206567",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000224",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206567",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000224",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206567r1043181_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206567r1043181",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000224-DB-000384",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must maintain the authenticity of communications sessions by guarding against man-in-the-middle attacks that guess at Session ID values.",
          "fix_text": "Utilize a DBMS product that can provide demonstrably effective protection against man-in-the-middle attacks that guess at session identifier values.\n\nConfigure DBMS settings to enable protections against man-in-the-middle attacks that guess at session identifier values.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS vendor documentation and system behavior (and if necessary, consult vendor representatives) to determine whether the DBMS can provide demonstrably effective protection against man-in-the-middle attacks that guess at session identifier values.\n\nIf not, this is a finding.\n\nReview DBMS settings to determine whether protections against man-in-the-middle attacks that guess at session identifier values are enabled.\n\nIf they are not, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "One class of man-in-the-middle, or session hijacking, attack involves the adversary guessing at valid session identifiers based on patterns in identifiers already known.\n\nThe preferred technique for thwarting guesses at Session IDs is the generation of unique session identifiers using a FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 approved random number generator.\n\nHowever, it is recognized that available DBMS products do not all implement the preferred technique yet may have other protections against session hijacking. Therefore, other techniques are acceptable, provided they are demonstrated to be effective.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72595",
            "V-58165"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001188"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "771ea68d-9b54-595e-b5d2-243a954fe7f1",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206568",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206568",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000225",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206568",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000225",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206568r961122_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206568r961122",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000225-DB-000153",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must fail to a secure state if system initialization fails, shutdown fails, or aborts fail.",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS settings so that, in the event of a system failure, the DBMS will roll back open transactions to a consistent state, to include a security configuration that is at least as restrictive as before the system failure.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS settings and vendor documentation to verify the DBMS properly handles transactions in the event of a system failure.\n\nIf open transactions are not rolled back to a consistent state during system failure, this is a finding.\n\nThe consistent state must include a security configuration that is at least as restrictive as before the system failure. If this is not guaranteed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Failure to a known state can address safety or security in accordance with the mission/business needs of the organization. \n\nFailure to a known secure state helps prevent a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability in the event of a failure of the information system or a component of the system. \n\nFailure to a known safe state helps prevent systems from failing to a state that may cause loss of data or unauthorized access to system resources. Systems that fail suddenly and with no incorporated failure state planning may leave the hosting system available but with a reduced security protection capability. Preserving information system state data also facilitates system restart and return to the operational mode of the organization with less disruption of mission/business processes. \n\nDatabases must fail to a known consistent state. Transactions must be successfully completed or rolled back.\n\nIn general, security mechanisms should be designed so that a failure will follow the same execution path as disallowing the operation. For example, application security methods, such as isAuthorized(), isAuthenticated(), and validate(), should all return false if there is an exception during processing. If security controls can throw exceptions, they must be very clear about exactly what that condition means. \n\nAbort refers to stopping a program or function before it has finished naturally. The term abort refers to both requested and unexpected terminations.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42865",
            "V-32528"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001190"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "ff3a513a-57b2-5f61-8722-8de251c4c3cf",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL supplies process-isolated backends, transactional/error semantics and authenticated connections; UDS restricts the container, and application input/error handling is explicit. Failed startup does not grant an authenticated SQL session.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[app-error] app-error\nUnknown/DB errors map to generic 500 problem details; HTTP transport and internal NATS dispatcher use the same mapper. Explicit application exceptions retain their supplied detail.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/errors/problem.mapper.ts\n\n[app-validation] app-validation\nGlobal interceptor validates declared params/query/headers/JSON with Zod, enforces Content-Type, and fails routes missing a documented exemption/schema; binary/multipart paths are handled separately.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/openapi/openapi.validation.interceptor.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL supplies process-isolated backends, transactional/error semantics and authenticated connections; UDS restricts the container, and application input/error handling is explicit. Failed startup does not grant an authenticated SQL session.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206569",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206569",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000226",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206569",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000226",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206569r961125_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206569r961125",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000226-DB-000147",
          "rule_title": "In the event of a system failure, the DBMS must preserve any information necessary to determine cause of failure and any information necessary to return to operations with least disruption to mission processes.",
          "fix_text": "Configure DBMS settings to preserve any organization-defined system state information in the event of a system failure.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS settings to determine whether organization-defined system state information is being preserved in the event of a system failure.\n\nIf organization-defined system state information is not being preserved, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Failure to a known state can address safety or security in accordance with the mission/business needs of the organization.\n\nFailure to a known secure state helps prevent a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability in the event of a failure of the information system or a component of the system. \n\nPreserving information system state information helps to facilitate system restart and return to the operational mode of the organization with less disruption of mission/business processes. \n\nSince it is usually not possible to test this capability in a production environment, systems should either be validated in a testing environment or prior to installation. This requirement is usually a function of the design of the IDPS component. Compliance can be verified by acceptance/validation processes or vendor attestation.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42866",
            "V-32529"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001665"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "8e1b61c2-b422-546f-a4dc-8eef26ec3d8a",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Persistent PGDATA retains native WAL for crash recovery while Vector keeps diagnostic logs off-process. The official check asks preservation of failure/recovery state, not an independent backup/PITR schedule.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain WAL/persistence and offloaded diagnostic source evidence; assess independent data backup under its actual backup controls.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-postgres-pod] re-database-postgres-pod\nDedicated PostgreSQL container/PVC and ClusterIP SQL service; PGDATA persists separately from read-only image. Native credentials from a managed Secret; generated initdb config in PGDATA. No human SQL login route is publicly exposed by Package.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[re-database-pg-wal] re-database-pg-wal\nNative WAL preserves crash-recovery state in durable data storage; independent backup/PITR is a separate recovery capability and not required merely to preserve diagnostics/state on a process failure.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-intro.html\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Persistent PGDATA retains native WAL for crash recovery while Vector keeps diagnostic logs off-process. The official check asks preservation of failure/recovery state, not an independent backup/PITR schedule.\n\nNext action: Retain WAL/persistence and offloaded diagnostic source evidence; assess independent data backup under its actual backup controls."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206570",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206570",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000231",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206570",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000231",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206570r961128_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206570r961128",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000231-DB-000154",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must protect the confidentiality and integrity of all information at rest.",
          "fix_text": "Apply appropriate controls to protect the confidentiality and integrity of data at rest in the database.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the application owner and Authorizing Official have determined that encryption of data at rest is NOT required, this is not a finding.\n\nReview DBMS settings to determine whether controls exist to protect the confidentiality and integrity of data at rest in the database.\n\nIf controls do not exist or are not enabled, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "This control is intended to address the confidentiality and integrity of information at rest in non-mobile devices and covers user information and system information. Information at rest refers to the state of information when it is located on a secondary storage device (e.g., disk drive, tape drive) within an organizational information system. Applications and application users generate information throughout the course of their application use. \n\nUser data generated, as well as application-specific configuration data, needs to be protected. Organizations may choose to employ different mechanisms to achieve confidentiality and integrity protections, as appropriate. \n\nIf the confidentiality and integrity of application data is not protected, the data will be open to compromise and unauthorized modification.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42871",
            "V-32534"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001199"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "78038c58-83f6-5fba-bde6-96d9334e80ba",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-postgres-pvc] sol-storage-postgres-pvc\nPostgreSQL mounts PGDATA on its own PVC and renders persistence.storageClassName when nonempty. Correct chart key is storageClassName; source/values/postgres.yaml uses storageClass, so changing that unmatched field would not select an encrypted class.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-chart] sol-storage-seaweed-chart\nCurrent chart runs weed server -s3 -s3.config=... without data-encryption flags; a mounted dataPVC supplies its persistence. Its PVC template currently has no storageClassName field, so add optional class binding or deliberately choose the cluster default.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/seaweedfs/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-pg-official] sol-storage-pg-official\nPostgreSQL18 explicitly supports filesystem/block/full-disk encryption as a data-partition protection layer. Mounted filesystem access still returns plaintext; this does not defeat privileged server access or replace authorization/integrity controls.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/encryption-options.html\n\n[sol-storage-core-boundary] sol-storage-core-boundary\nCore expects an existing cluster and defaultStorageClass; it does not provision MPN host disk encryption. Gallatin owns this infrastructure choice and can bind the app/CorePVCs to a selected encrypted storage class.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/prerequisites.mdx\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity] sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity\nLinux dm-integrity with dm-crypt supports authenticated disk encryption that rejects modified encrypted sectors with I/O error. Standalone CRC mode only detects accidental corruption; journal/mode choices affect consistency and performance. Distribution/FIPS-module support must be selected, not assumed.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-integrity.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206571",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206571",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000233",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206571",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000233",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206571r961131_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206571r961131",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000233-DB-000124",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must isolate security functions from non-security functions.",
          "fix_text": "Locate security-related database objects and code in a separate database, schema, or other separate security domain from database objects and code implementing application logic.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Check DBMS settings to determine whether objects or code implementing security functionality are located in a separate security domain, such as a separate database or schema created specifically for security functionality.\n\nIf security-related database objects or code are not kept separate, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "An isolation boundary provides access control and protects the integrity of the hardware, software, and firmware that perform security functions. \n\nSecurity functions are the hardware, software, and/or firmware of the information system responsible for enforcing the system security policy and supporting the isolation of code and data on which the protection is based.\n\nDevelopers and implementers can increase the assurance in security functions by employing well-defined security policy models; structured, disciplined, and rigorous hardware and software development techniques; and sound system/security engineering principles. \n\nDatabase Management Systems typically separate security functionality from non-security functionality via separate databases or schemas. Database objects or code implementing security functionality should not be commingled with objects or code implementing application logic. When security and non-security functionality are commingled, users who have access to non-security functionality may be able to access security functionality.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42873",
            "V-32536"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001084"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "ef63f9c0-5eb6-51a6-925c-e8626751bdb1",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206572",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206572",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000243",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206572",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000243",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206572r1137656_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206572r1137656",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000243-DB-000128",
          "rule_title": "Database contents must be protected from unauthorized and unintended information transfer by enforcement of a data-transfer policy.",
          "fix_text": "Modify any code used for moving data from production to development/test systems to comply with the organization-defined data transfer policy, and to ensure copies of production data are not left in unsecured locations.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the procedures for the refreshing of development/test data from production.\n\nReview any scripts or code that exists for the movement of production data to development/test systems, or to any other location or for any other purpose.\n\nVerify that copies of production data are not left in unprotected locations. \n\nIf the code that exists for data movement does not comply with the organization-defined data transfer policy and/or fails to remove any copies of production data from unprotected locations, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Applications, including DBMSs, must prevent unauthorized and unintended information transfer via shared system resources. \n\nData used for the development and testing of applications often involves copying data from production. It is important that specific procedures exist for this process, to include the conditions under which such transfer may take place, where the copies may reside, and the rules for ensuring sensitive data are not exposed.\n\nCopies of sensitive data must not be misplaced or left in a temporary location without the proper controls.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-42884",
            "V-32547"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001090"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "f79495b5-3a8b-526d-8043-27abde6e258a",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL role separation, application tenant/actor context and container process/PVC isolation constrain unintended data transfer. The final approved flow policy and security-label taxonomy are separate policy requirements.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL role separation, application tenant/actor context and container process/PVC isolation constrain unintended data transfer. The final approved flow policy and security-label taxonomy are separate policy requirements.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206573",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206573",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000243",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206573",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000243",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206573r1137657_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206573r1137657",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000243-DB-000373",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must prevent unauthorized and unintended information transfer via shared system resources.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of effectively protecting the private resources of one process or user from unauthorized access by another user or process.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to effectively protect the private resources of one process or user from unauthorized access by another user or process.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the DBMS architecture to find out if and how it protects the private resources of one process or user (such as working memory, temporary tables, uncommitted data) from unauthorized access by another user or process.\n\nIf it does not effectively do so, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The purpose of this control is to prevent information, including encrypted representations of information, produced by the actions of a prior user/role (or the actions of a process acting on behalf of a prior user/role) from being available to any current user/role (or current process) that obtains access to a shared system resource (e.g., registers, main memory, secondary storage) after the resource has been released back to the information system. Control of information in shared resources is also referred to as object reuse.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72579",
            "V-58149"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001090"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "0398f8d4-7192-5ae6-88e8-add2356b5ae3",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL role separation, application tenant/actor context and container process/PVC isolation constrain unintended data transfer. The final approved flow policy and security-label taxonomy are separate policy requirements.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[authz] Application access control\nAPI guard validates identity and sets tenant/user context; permission guard invokes a permission enforcer when route metadata is present. This is source support, not complete route/tenant coverage.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/api/auth/auth.guard.ts\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL role separation, application tenant/actor context and container process/PVC isolation constrain unintended data transfer. The final approved flow policy and security-label taxonomy are separate policy requirements.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
