        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206591",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206591",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000357",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206591",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000357",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206591r961392_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206591r961392",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000357-DB-000316",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must allocate audit record storage capacity in accordance with organization-defined audit record storage requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Allocate sufficient audit file/table space to support peak demand.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Investigate whether there have been any incidents where the DBMS ran out of audit log space since the last time the space was allocated or other corrective measures were taken.\n\nIf there have been, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In order to ensure sufficient storage capacity for the audit logs, the DBMS must be able to allocate audit record storage capacity. Although another requirement (SRG-APP-000515-DB-000318) mandates that audit data be off-loaded to a centralized log management system, it remains necessary to provide space on the database server to serve as a buffer against outages and capacity limits of the off-loading mechanism.\n\nThe task of allocating audit record storage capacity is usually performed during initial installation of the DBMS and is closely associated with the DBA and system administrator roles. The DBA or system administrator will usually coordinate the allocation of physical drive space with the application owner/installer and the application will prompt the installer to provide the capacity information, the physical location of the disk, or both.\n\nIn determining the capacity requirements, consider such factors as: total number of users; expected number of concurrent users during busy periods; number and type of events being monitored; types and amounts of data being captured; the frequency/speed with which audit records are off-loaded to the central log management system; and any limitations that exist on the DBMS's ability to reuse the space formerly occupied by off-loaded records.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72483",
            "V-58053"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001849"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "cfab63a2-8582-5574-a81b-0649f4e71536",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Database stderr logs are collected into Vector's explicit acknowledged disk buffer and centralized Loki storage, rather than consuming only the database data volume. Final object-store capacity must be sized to the chosen event catalog.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Database stderr logs are collected into Vector's explicit acknowledged disk buffer and centralized Loki storage, rather than consuming only the database data volume. Final object-store capacity must be sized to the chosen event catalog.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206592",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206592",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000359",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206592",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000359",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206592r961398_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206592r961398",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000359-DB-000319",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must provide a warning to appropriate support staff when allocated audit record storage volume reaches 75% of maximum audit record storage capacity.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the system to notify appropriate support staff immediately upon storage volume utilization reaching 75%.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system configuration.\n\nIf appropriate support staff are not notified immediately upon storage volume utilization reaching 75%, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Organizations are required to use a central log management system, so, under normal conditions, the audit space allocated to the DBMS on its own server will not be an issue. However, space will still be required on the DBMS server for audit records in transit, and, under abnormal conditions, this could fill up. Since a requirement exists to halt processing upon audit failure, a service outage would result.\n\nIf support personnel are not notified immediately upon storage volume utilization reaching 75%, they are unable to plan for storage capacity expansion. \n\nThe appropriate support staff include, at a minimum, the ISSO and the DBA/SA.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72487",
            "V-58057"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001855"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "d6a79d1f-a835-5bd3-bd2e-f1b1cf0287d7",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides collector/storage monitoring and runtime detections, so no DB-local alert engine is inherently needed. However, the selected package source still does not configure the exact audit75% threshold and required real-time audit-failure/tamper routes to support roles. General metrics/detections alone do not identify a configured notification path. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the assessed audit/log repository and capacity metric; configure an immediate warning at 75 percent utilization to the recipients specified by this exact rule. Use a different threshold only where this rule expressly permits it and the required approval is retained. Test threshold triggering and delivery. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-alerts] re-database-alerts\nCore enables general cluster/storage monitoring; candidate source does not add a DB audit-volume75% rule or specific audit-failure/tamper notification receiver.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-database-log-access] re-database-log-access\nUDS Grafana disables local login and maps identity groups to Admin/Viewer; protected logs can supply privileged review without DBMS-local audit dashboard.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides collector/storage monitoring and runtime detections, so no DB-local alert engine is inherently needed. However, the selected package source still does not configure the exact audit75% threshold and required real-time audit-failure/tamper routes to support roles. General metrics/detections alone do not identify a configured notification path. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the assessed audit/log repository and capacity metric; configure an immediate warning at 75 percent utilization to the recipients specified by this exact rule. Use a different threshold only where this rule expressly permits it and the required approval is retained. Test threshold triggering and delivery. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206593",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206593",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000360",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206593",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000360",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206593r961401_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206593r961401",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000360-DB-000320",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must provide an immediate real-time alert to appropriate support staff of all audit log failures.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the system to provide immediate real-time alerts to appropriate support staff when an audit log failure occurs.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS settings, OS, or third-party logging software settings to determine whether a real-time alert will be sent to the appropriate personnel when auditing fails for any reason.\n\nIf real-time alerts are not sent upon auditing failure, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "It is critical for the appropriate personnel to be aware if a system is at risk of failing to process audit logs as required. Without a real-time alert, security personnel may be unaware of an impending failure of the audit capability, and system operation may be adversely affected. \n\nThe appropriate support staff include, at a minimum, the ISSO and the DBA/SA.\n\nA failure of database auditing will result in either the database continuing to function without auditing or in a complete halt to database operations. When audit processing fails, appropriate personnel must be alerted immediately to avoid further downtime or unaudited transactions.\n\nAlerts provide organizations with urgent messages. Real-time alerts provide these messages immediately (i.e., the time from event detection to alert occurs in seconds or less).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72489",
            "V-58059"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001858"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "e84ca339-fb98-537a-9f53-db093af4d0d0",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides collector/storage monitoring and runtime detections, so no DB-local alert engine is inherently needed. However, the selected package source still does not configure the exact audit75% threshold and required real-time audit-failure/tamper routes to support roles. General metrics/detections alone do not identify a configured notification path. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-alerts] re-database-alerts\nCore enables general cluster/storage monitoring; candidate source does not add a DB audit-volume75% rule or specific audit-failure/tamper notification receiver.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-database-log-access] re-database-log-access\nUDS Grafana disables local login and maps identity groups to Admin/Viewer; protected logs can supply privileged review without DBMS-local audit dashboard.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides collector/storage monitoring and runtime detections, so no DB-local alert engine is inherently needed. However, the selected package source still does not configure the exact audit75% threshold and required real-time audit-failure/tamper routes to support roles. General metrics/detections alone do not identify a configured notification path. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify the audit/log processing failures covered by this exact rule, including capture, hardware/software, collector/backend and storage failures as applicable. Configure the required alerts and named recipients using the existing central monitoring. Test coverage and delivery; verify real-time latency where the rule requires it. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206594",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206594",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000374",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206594",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000374",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206594r961443_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206594r961443",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000374-DB-000322",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must record time stamps, in audit records and application data, that can be mapped to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC, formerly GMT).",
          "fix_text": "Ensure the DBMS generates time stamps, in audit records and application data, that maps to UTC.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify that the DBMS generates time stamps, in audit records and application data, that maps to UTC.\n\nIf it does not, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If time stamps are not consistently applied and there is no common time reference, it is difficult to perform forensic analysis.\n\nTime stamps generated by the DBMS must include date and time. Time is commonly expressed in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), a modern continuation of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT), or local time with an offset from UTC. \n\nSome DBMS products offer a data type called TIMESTAMP that is not a representation of date and time. Rather, it is a database state counter and does not correspond to calendar and clock time. This requirement does not refer to that meaning of TIMESTAMP.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72493",
            "V-58063"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001890"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "e16f355c-c254-5ce2-8f93-b77d64c6b86b",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL server logging and timestamptz application audit fields use system time with subsecond precision; containers inherit MPN node time, and Vector preserves/labels the records.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-permission-table] app-permission-table\nAppend-only role-grant/revocation table includes actor user, subject user, tenant, role, action and timestamp; it does not record every request or failed authorization.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL server logging and timestamptz application audit fields use system time with subsecond precision; containers inherit MPN node time, and Vector preserves/labels the records.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206595",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206595",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000375",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206595",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000375",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206595r961446_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206595r961446",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000375-DB-000323",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate time stamps, for audit records and application data, with a minimum granularity of one second.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS that can generate and record time stamps with a granularity of one second or finer.\n\nConfigure auditing so that the time stamps are recorded to a precision of one second or finer.\n\nModify applications and/or column/field definitions so that the time stamps in audit trail columns/fields in application data are recorded to a precision of one second or finer.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review product documentation to verify that the DBMS can generate time stamps with a granularity of one second or finer. If it cannot, this is a finding.\n\nReview audit log records produced by the DBMS for confirmation that time stamps are recorded to a precision of one second or finer. If not, this is a finding.\n\nReview time stamp values in audit trail columns/fields in application data in the database. If the time stamps are not recorded to a precision of one second or finer, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without sufficient granularity of time stamps, it is not possible to adequately determine the chronological order of records. \n\nTime stamps generated by the DBMS must include date and time. Granularity of time measurements refers to the precision available in time stamp values. Granularity coarser than one second is not sufficient for audit trail purposes. Time stamp values are typically presented with three or more decimal places of seconds; however, the actual granularity may be coarser than the apparent precision. For example, SQL Server's GETDATE()/CURRENT_TMESTAMP values are presented to three decimal places, but the granularity is not one millisecond: it is about 1/300 of a second.\n\nSome DBMS products offer a data type called TIMESTAMP that is not a representation of date and time. Rather, it is a database state counter and does not correspond to calendar and clock time. This requirement does not refer to that meaning of TIMESTAMP.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72495",
            "V-58065"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001889"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "83ae3a12-7aab-59be-9f90-ec1f0de1a01a",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL server logging and timestamptz application audit fields use system time with subsecond precision; containers inherit MPN node time, and Vector preserves/labels the records.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-permission-table] app-permission-table\nAppend-only role-grant/revocation table includes actor user, subject user, tenant, role, action and timestamp; it does not record every request or failed authorization.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL server logging and timestamptz application audit fields use system time with subsecond precision; containers inherit MPN node time, and Vector preserves/labels the records.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206596",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206596",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000378",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206596",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000378",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206596r981956_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206596r981956",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000378-DB-000365",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must prohibit user installation of logic modules (stored procedures, functions, triggers, views, etc.) without explicit privileged status.",
          "fix_text": "Document and obtain approval for any nonadministrative users who require the ability to create, alter or replace logic modules.\n\nImplement the approved permissions. Revoke any unapproved permissions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS supports only software development, experimentation and/or developer-level testing (that is, excluding production systems, integration testing, stress testing, and user acceptance testing), this is not a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS and database security settings with respect to nonadministrative users' ability to create, alter, or replace logic modules, to include but not necessarily only stored procedures, functions, triggers, and views.\n\nIf any such permissions exist and are not documented and approved, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Allowing regular users to install software, without explicit privileges, creates the risk that untested or potentially malicious software will be installed on the system. Explicit privileges (escalated or administrative privileges) provide the regular user with explicit capabilities and control that exceed the rights of a regular user.\n\nDBMS functionality and the nature and requirements of databases will vary; so while users are not permitted to install unapproved software, there may be instances where the organization allows the user to install approved software packages such as from an approved software repository. The requirements for production servers will be more restrictive than those used for development and research.\n\nThe DBMS must enforce software installation by users based upon what types of software installations are permitted (e.g., updates and security patches to existing software) and what types of installations are prohibited (e.g., software whose pedigree with regard to being potentially malicious is unknown or suspect) by the organization). \n\nIn the case of a database management system, this requirement covers stored procedures, functions, triggers, views, etc.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72565",
            "V-58135"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003980"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "290e67f7-ad6f-5f7c-af4e-63d52e34ede7",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206597",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206597",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000380",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206597",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000380",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206597r961461_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206597r961461",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000380-DB-000360",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must enforce access restrictions associated with changes to the configuration of the DBMS or database(s).",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of enforcing access restrictions associated with changes to the configuration of the DBMS or database(s).\n\nConfigure the DBMS to enforce access restrictions associated with changes to the configuration of the DBMS or database(s).",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS vendor documentation with respect to its ability to enforce access restrictions associated with changes to the configuration of the DBMS or database(s).\n\nIf it is not able to do this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the security configuration of the DBMS and database(s).\n\nIf it does not enforce access restrictions associated with changes to the configuration of the DBMS or database(s), this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Failure to provide logical access restrictions associated with changes to configuration may have significant effects on the overall security of the system. \n\nWhen dealing with access restrictions pertaining to change control, it should be noted that any changes to the hardware, software, and/or firmware components of the information system can potentially have significant effects on the overall security of the system. \n\nAccordingly, only qualified and authorized individuals should be allowed to obtain access to system components for the purposes of initiating changes, including upgrades and modifications.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72555",
            "V-58125"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001813"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "c7258404-43c1-55a6-ba3e-b18b3d99fe37",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-role] app-db-role\nhorizon_app is a distinct LOGIN role granted application DML and schema usage, not superuser/DDL by this migration; migrations/admin credentials are separate.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-policy] core-policy\nPepr mutates/validates non-root execution, no privilege escalation and dropped capabilities, including init and ephemeral containers; exemptions are explicit.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/policies/security.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL native roles/privilege checks and the separate horizon_app DML role restrict schema/administrative operations; non-root PGDATA isolation and UDS policy protect the process/files. SECURITY DEFINER functions are deliberate privileged modules and require an allow-list review, not a claim that PostgreSQL lacks access-control enforcement.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
