          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "eb256e5c-28b0-5926-ba33-3f2101fa9966",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL has an internal ClusterIP service with declared application port5432 and no candidate public DB ingress; mesh/default-deny govern connectivity. Final approved PPSM/profile mapping remains required.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL has an internal ClusterIP service with declared application port5432 and no candidate public DB ingress; mesh/default-deny govern connectivity. Final approved PPSM/profile mapping remains required.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206600",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206600",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000389",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206600",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000389",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206600r1268170_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206600r1268170",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000389-DB-000372",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must require users to reauthenticate when organization-defined circumstances or situations require reauthentication.",
          "fix_text": "Modify and/or configure the DBMS and related applications and tools so that users are always required to reauthenticate when changing role or escalating privileges.\n\nModify and/or configure the DBMS and related applications and tools so that users are always required to reauthenticate when the specified cases needing reauthorization occur.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the system documentation and the configuration of the DBMS and related applications and tools.\n\nIf there are any circumstances under which a user is not required to reauthenticate when changing role or escalating privileges, this is a finding.\n\nIf the information owner has identified additional cases where reauthentication is needed, but there are circumstances where the system does not ask the user to reauthenticate when those cases occur, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The DoW standard for authentication of an interactive user is the presentation of a common access card (CAC) or other physical token bearing a valid, current, DoW-issued Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificate, coupled with a Personal Identification Number (PIN) to be entered by the user at the beginning of each session and whenever reauthentication is required.\n\nWithout reauthentication, users may access resources or perform tasks for which they do not have authorization. \n\nWhen applications provide the capability to change security roles or escalate the functional capability of the application, it is critical the user reauthenticate.\n\nIn addition to the reauthentication requirements associated with session locks, organizations may require reauthentication of individuals and/or devices in other situations, including (but not limited to) the following circumstances:\n\n(i) When authenticators change; \n(ii) When roles change; \n(iii) When security categories of information systems change; \n(iv) When the execution of privileged functions occurs; \n(v) After a fixed period of time; or\n(vi) Periodically.\n\nWithin the DoW, the minimum circumstances requiring reauthentication are privilege escalation and role changes.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72577",
            "V-58147"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002038"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "27655231-e9af-5c39-8990-054a840d2927",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Normal product users cannot issue SET ROLE/admin escalation through the application; their identity is validated by shared UDS and API permission checks. Native roles are fixed service identities. The official mandatory reauthentication scenario is a user role escalation, so record this noninteractive boundary and the separate privileged operator path instead of adding pointless MFA prompts to pooled SQL.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Document fixed service-role/no-interactive-escalation applicability and inherited administrator reauthentication; reassess any human direct SQL login introduced.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-package-network] re-database-package-network\nUDS ambient mesh enrolls horizon namespace and controls SQL connectivity through namespace policy. Public exposes API/web/NATS and bundledKeycloak only when selected; no public PostgreSQL endpoint.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Normal product users cannot issue SET ROLE/admin escalation through the application; their identity is validated by shared UDS and API permission checks. Native roles are fixed service identities. The official mandatory reauthentication scenario is a user role escalation, so record this noninteractive boundary and the separate privileged operator path instead of adding pointless MFA prompts to pooled SQL.\n\nNext action: Document fixed service-role/no-interactive-escalation applicability and inherited administrator reauthentication; reassess any human direct SQL login introduced."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206601",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206601",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000400",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206601",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000400",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206601r961521_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206601r961521",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000400-DB-000367",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must prohibit the use of cached authenticators after an organization-defined time period.",
          "fix_text": "Modify system settings to implement the organization-defined limit on the lifetime of cached authenticators.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system settings to determine whether the organization-defined limit for cached authentication is implemented.\n\nIf it is not implemented, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "If cached authentication information is out-of-date, the validity of the authentication information may be questionable.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72567",
            "V-58137"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002007"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "bade086a-a378-5cd9-af13-99dde0d32aa3",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Native DB service authentication uses a password verifier at connection establishment, not a configured offline cache of previously successful human authentications. Long-lived SQL pooling is a service-session lifecycle issue, not proof of an authentication-cache bypass. Human token expiry/revalidation belongs to the selected UDS/API boundary.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record no native cached-human-authentication applicability and link the actual UDS/API expiry policy; reassess if an offline credential cache is introduced.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-config] re-database-config\nOpenTelemetry tracing defaults false, and SQL SSL default disabled under intended ambient mesh. No native DB certificate-auth/cache mechanism is configured.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/config/config.schema.ts\n\n[re-database-query-context] re-database-query-context\nAPI service uses database pool with12-second statement_timeout and logs startup/close. This limits query duration, not DB per-role concurrent sessions or all connection idle lifetime.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/database/database.service.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Native DB service authentication uses a password verifier at connection establishment, not a configured offline cache of previously successful human authentications. Long-lived SQL pooling is a service-session lifecycle issue, not proof of an authentication-cache bypass. Human token expiry/revalidation belongs to the selected UDS/API boundary.\n\nNext action: Record no native cached-human-authentication applicability and link the actual UDS/API expiry policy; reassess if an offline credential cache is introduced."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206603",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206603",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000427",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206603",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000427",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206603r1268173_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206603r1268173",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000427-DB-000385",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must only accept end entity certificates issued by DoW Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) or DoW-approved PKI Certification Authorities (CAs) for the establishment of all encrypted sessions.",
          "fix_text": "Revoke trust in any certificates not issued by a DoW-approved certificate authority. Configure the DBMS to accept only DoW and DoW-approved PKI end-entity certificates.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS will accept non-DoW approved PKI end-entity certificates, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Only DoW-approved external PKIs have been evaluated to ensure that they have security controls and identity vetting procedures in place that are sufficient for DoW systems to rely on the identity asserted in the certificate. PKIs lacking sufficient security controls and identity vetting procedures risk being compromised and risk issuing certificates that enable adversaries to impersonate legitimate users. \n\nThe authoritative list of DoW-approved PKIs is published at https://www.cyber.mil/pki-pke.\n\nThis requirement focuses on communications protection for the DBMS session rather than for the network packet.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72597",
            "V-58167"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002470"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "634b703e-ab06-5696-bda2-733133a8d09b",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL native PKI authentication/TLS trust-store functionality is not selected; password-authenticated SQL runs inside UDS ambient mutual TLS. Certificate issuance/trust is therefore assessed at the UDS mesh/gateway boundary. Do not require a second PostgreSQL trust store; attach accepted private-workload-PKI/approved-CA delegation for the actual encrypted path.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Record DB-native PKI nonapplicability and map approved trust-anchor requirements to UDS mesh/operator certificate evidence.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-config] re-database-config\nOpenTelemetry tracing defaults false, and SQL SSL default disabled under intended ambient mesh. No native DB certificate-auth/cache mechanism is configured.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/config/config.schema.ts\n\n[re-database-mtls] re-database-mtls\nUDS root STRICT mesh policy authenticates/encrypts enrolled workload hops independently of PostgreSQL native TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[re-database-package-network] re-database-package-network\nUDS ambient mesh enrolls horizon namespace and controls SQL connectivity through namespace policy. Public exposes API/web/NATS and bundledKeycloak only when selected; no public PostgreSQL endpoint.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml\n\n[re-database-storage] re-database-storage\nUDS provisions external storage/operator services and supplied trust material; source does not identify actual MPN disk encryption settings or approved roots. Do not substitute unrelated Gallatin AWS infrastructure for MPN.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/provision-services.mdx",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. PostgreSQL native PKI authentication/TLS trust-store functionality is not selected; password-authenticated SQL runs inside UDS ambient mutual TLS. Certificate issuance/trust is therefore assessed at the UDS mesh/gateway boundary. Do not require a second PostgreSQL trust store; attach accepted private-workload-PKI/approved-CA delegation for the actual encrypted path.\n\nNext action: Record DB-native PKI nonapplicability and map approved trust-anchor requirements to UDS mesh/operator certificate evidence."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206604",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206604",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000428",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206604",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000428",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206604r1018552_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206604r1018552",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000428-DB-000386",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized modification of organization-defined information at rest (to include, at a minimum, PII and classified information) on organization-defined information system components.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS, operating system/file system, and additional software as relevant, to provide the required level of cryptographic protection.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the system documentation to determine whether the organization has defined the information at rest that is to be protected from modification, which must include, at a minimum, PII and classified information.\n\nIf no information is identified as requiring such protection, this is not a finding.\n\nReview the configuration of the DBMS, operating system/file system, and additional software as relevant.\n\nIf any of the information defined as requiring cryptographic protection from modification is not encrypted in a manner that provides the required level of protection, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "DBMSs handling data requiring \"data at rest\" protections must employ cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure and modification of the information at rest. These cryptographic mechanisms may be native to the DBMS or implemented via additional software or operating system/file system settings, as appropriate to the situation.\n\nSelection of a cryptographic mechanism is based on the need to protect the integrity of organizational information. The strength of the mechanism is commensurate with the security category and/or classification of the information. Organizations have the flexibility to either encrypt all information on storage devices (i.e., full disk encryption) or encrypt specific data structures (e.g., files, records, or fields). \n\nThe decision whether and what to encrypt rests with the data owner and is also influenced by the physical measures taken to secure the equipment and media on which the information resides.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
