          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "ccdf068f-9a4d-5fd9-9c37-3915d09fa408",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for cryptographic integrity. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for cryptographic integrity. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-pg-official] sol-storage-pg-official\nPostgreSQL18 explicitly supports filesystem/block/full-disk encryption as a data-partition protection layer. Mounted filesystem access still returns plaintext; this does not defeat privileged server access or replace authorization/integrity controls.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/encryption-options.html\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity] sol-storage-dm-auth-integrity\nLinux dm-integrity with dm-crypt supports authenticated disk encryption that rejects modified encrypted sectors with I/O error. Standalone CRC mode only detects accidental corruption; journal/mode choices affect consistency and performance. Distribution/FIPS-module support must be selected, not assumed.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-integrity.html\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-aead] sol-storage-seaweed-aead\nExact delivered SeaweedFS4.52 cipher code generates32-byte random keys, AES-GCM encryption/nonces and authenticated gcm.Open decryption. This is an existing object-storage mechanism, not new API cryptography.\nhttps://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs/blob/4.52/weed/util/cipher.go\n\n[sol-storage-dm-auth-modes] sol-storage-dm-auth-modes\nKernel dm-crypt documents authenticated modes such as gcm(aes) and authenc(hmac(sha256),xts(aes)) using integrity:aead metadata. Plain AES-XTS is not this authenticated mode. Existing host functionality can supply integrity without adding custom DB crypto, when supported in the selected approved platform.\nhttps://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/admin-guide/device-mapper/dm-crypt.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for cryptographic integrity. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for cryptographic integrity. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206605",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206605",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000429",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206605",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000429",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206605r1018553_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206605r1018553",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000429-DB-000387",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must implement cryptographic mechanisms preventing the unauthorized disclosure of organization-defined information at rest on organization-defined information system components.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS, operating system/file system, and additional software as relevant, to provide the required level of cryptographic protection for information requiring cryptographic protection against disclosure.\n\nSecure the premises, equipment, and media to provide the required level of physical protection.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the system documentation to determine whether the organization has defined the information at rest that is to be protected from disclosure, which must include, at a minimum, PII and classified information.\n\nIf the documentation indicates no information requires such protections, this is not a finding.\n\nReview the configuration of the DBMS, operating system/file system, and additional software as relevant.\n\nIf any of the information defined as requiring protection is not encrypted in a manner that provides the required level of protection and is not physically secured to the required level, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "DBMSs handling data requiring \"data at rest\" protections must employ cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure and modification of the information at rest. These cryptographic mechanisms may be native to the DBMS or implemented via additional software or operating system/file system settings, as appropriate to the situation.\n\nSelection of a cryptographic mechanism is based on the need to protect the integrity of organizational information. The strength of the mechanism is commensurate with the security category and/or classification of the information. Organizations have the flexibility to either encrypt all information on storage devices (i.e., full disk encryption) or encrypt specific data structures (e.g., files, records, or fields). \n\nThe decision whether and what to encrypt rests with the data owner and is also influenced by the physical measures taken to secure the equipment and media on which the information resides.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72601",
            "V-58171"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002476"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "eb3c6f36-f078-5a06-896d-efe30fd7567d",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: MPN/platform storage owner (proposed; reviewer confirmation required); Gallatin for application requirements/configuration and residuals\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification.\n\nEvidence:\n[sol-storage-postgres-pvc] sol-storage-postgres-pvc\nPostgreSQL mounts PGDATA on its own PVC and renders persistence.storageClassName when nonempty. Correct chart key is storageClassName; source/values/postgres.yaml uses storageClass, so changing that unmatched field would not select an encrypted class.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/templates/statefulset.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-seaweed-chart] sol-storage-seaweed-chart\nCurrent chart runs weed server -s3 -s3.config=... without data-encryption flags; a mounted dataPVC supplies its persistence. Its PVC template currently has no storageClassName field, so add optional class binding or deliberately choose the cluster default.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/seaweedfs/templates/deployment.yaml\n\n[sol-storage-pg-official] sol-storage-pg-official\nPostgreSQL18 explicitly supports filesystem/block/full-disk encryption as a data-partition protection layer. Mounted filesystem access still returns plaintext; this does not defeat privileged server access or replace authorization/integrity controls.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/encryption-options.html\n\n[sol-storage-core-boundary] sol-storage-core-boundary\nCore expects an existing cluster and defaultStorageClass; it does not provision MPN host disk encryption. Gallatin owns this infrastructure choice and can bind the app/CorePVCs to a selected encrypted storage class.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/docs/getting-started/production/prerequisites.mdx\n\n[sol-storage-k8-encryption] sol-storage-k8-encryption\nKubernetes Secret storage is plaintext unless API server uses an EncryptionConfiguration with a nonidentity provider first. Configure --encryption-provider-config or the distribution equivalent; existing values need rewriting to migrate stored Secret data after enablement.\nhttps://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Human review on 2026-11-10 selected provisional Likely covered through inherited MPN/platform storage controls under the instruction to apply the same recommendation to similar storage-protection requirements. PR reviewer must confirm or reject platform ownership and the mapping for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. Actual platform protection and the authoritative responsibility boundary remain unverified; this is not an accepted compliance result or an N/A determination. Gallatin retains application data requirements, use of approved protected storage and any application/configuration residuals. This supersedes any earlier assumption that Gallatin must provision a new storage layer.\n\nNext action: PR reviewer must confirm or reject MPN/platform ownership and inherited coverage for storage confidentiality and integrity as required by the exact rule. In the post-classification verification pass, obtain the responsibility agreement and evidence for the actual protection, map all data/components covered by this exact rule, and verify Horizon uses the protected boundary correctly. Identify any Gallatin application/configuration residual separately and revise the recommendation if inheritance or protection is unsupported. Do not prescribe new storage infrastructure before that verification."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206606",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206606",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000431",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206606",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000431",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206606r1137659_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206606r1137659",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000431-DB-000388",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must maintain a separate execution domain for each executing process.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of maintaining a separate execution domain for each executing process.\n\nIf this is a configurable feature, configure the DBMS to implement it.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the DBMS architecture to find out if and how it protects the private resources of one process (such as working memory, temporary tables, uncommitted data and, especially, executable code) from unauthorized access or modification by another user or process.\n\nIf it is not capable of maintaining a separate execution domain for each executing process, this is a finding.\n\nIf the DBMS is capable of maintaining a separate execution domain for each executing process, but is configured not to do so, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Database management systems can maintain separate execution domains for each executing process by assigning each process a separate address space. Each process has a distinct address space so that communication between processes is controlled through the security functions, and one process cannot modify the executing code of another process. Maintaining separate execution domains for executing processes can be achieved, for example, by implementing separate address spaces.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72603",
            "V-58173"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002530"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "199b6569-a4fc-5646-af06-f2be17b2b3bd",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL supplies process-isolated backends, transactional/error semantics and authenticated connections; UDS restricts the container, and application input/error handling is explicit. Failed startup does not grant an authenticated SQL session.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[app-error] app-error\nUnknown/DB errors map to generic 500 problem details; HTTP transport and internal NATS dispatcher use the same mapper. Explicit application exceptions retain their supplied detail.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/errors/problem.mapper.ts\n\n[app-validation] app-validation\nGlobal interceptor validates declared params/query/headers/JSON with Zod, enforces Content-Type, and fails routes missing a documented exemption/schema; binary/multipart paths are handled separately.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/openapi/openapi.validation.interceptor.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL supplies process-isolated backends, transactional/error semantics and authenticated connections; UDS restricts the container, and application input/error handling is explicit. Failed startup does not grant an authenticated SQL session.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206607",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206607",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000441",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206607",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000441",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206607r961638_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206607r961638",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000441-DB-000378",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must maintain the confidentiality and integrity of information during preparation for transmission.",
          "fix_text": "Implement protective measures against unauthorized disclosure and modification during preparation for transmission.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the data owner does not have a strict requirement for ensuring data integrity and confidentiality is maintained at every step of the data transfer and handling process, this is not a finding.\n\nIf the DBMS does not employ protective measures against unauthorized disclosure and modification during preparation for transmission, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information can be either unintentionally or maliciously disclosed or modified during preparation for transmission, including, for example, during aggregation, at protocol transformation points, and during packing/unpacking. These unauthorized disclosures or modifications compromise the confidentiality or integrity of the information.\n\nUse of this requirement will be limited to situations where the data owner has a strict requirement for ensuring data integrity and confidentiality is maintained at every step of the data transfer and handling process. \n\nWhen transmitting data, the DBMS, associated applications, and infrastructure must leverage transmission protection mechanisms.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72583",
            "V-58153"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002420"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "84cff254-e5e2-55f0-be68-ae57a1bfc93a",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206608",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206608",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000442",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206608",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000442",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206608r961641_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206608r961641",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000442-DB-000379",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must maintain the confidentiality and integrity of information during reception.",
          "fix_text": "Implement protective measures against unauthorized disclosure and modification during reception.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the data owner does not have a strict requirement for ensuring data integrity and confidentiality is maintained at every step of the data transfer and handling process, this is not a finding.\n\nIf the DBMS, associated applications, and infrastructure do not employ protective measures against unauthorized disclosure and modification during reception, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information can be either unintentionally or maliciously disclosed or modified during reception, including, for example, during aggregation, at protocol transformation points, and during packing/unpacking. These unauthorized disclosures or modifications compromise the confidentiality or integrity of the information.\n\nThis requirement applies only to those applications that are either distributed or can allow access to data nonlocally. Use of this requirement will be limited to situations where the data owner has a strict requirement for ensuring data integrity and confidentiality is maintained at every step of the data transfer and handling process. \n\nWhen receiving data, the DBMS, associated applications, and infrastructure must leverage protection mechanisms.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
