          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "80d24ccf-50e7-5aaf-8154-aabb69dafe52",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-mtls] core-mtls\nRoot-namespace PeerAuthentication requires STRICT mTLS. Horizon opts into ambient mesh; in-mesh plaintext-looking URLs are protected by mesh rather than application TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[mesh] Mesh and policy\nPackage chart declares ambient mesh and generated network policy. Disabled chart-local NetworkPolicy and plaintext application URLs do not establish missing mesh enforcement.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Database clients and server are in the declared ambient mesh protected by root STRICT mTLS, so sslmode=disable is not proof of plaintext on the pod-to-pod wire. The approved FIPS module/trust-store claim is separate.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206609",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206609",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000447",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206609",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000447",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206609r961656_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206609r961656",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000447-DB-000393",
          "rule_title": "When invalid inputs are received, the DBMS must behave in a predictable and documented manner that reflects organizational and system objectives.",
          "fix_text": "Revise and deploy the source code for database program objects (stored procedures, functions, triggers) and application source code, to implement the documented behavior.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review system documentation to determine how input errors are to be handled in general and if any special handling is defined for specific circumstances.\n\nReview the source code for database program objects (stored procedures, functions, triggers) and application source code to identify how the system responds to invalid input.\n\nIf it does not implement the documented behavior, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "A common vulnerability is unplanned behavior when invalid inputs are received. This requirement guards against adverse or unintended system behavior caused by invalid inputs, where information system responses to the invalid input may be disruptive or cause the system to fail into an unsafe state.\n\nThe behavior will be derived from the organizational and system requirements and includes, but is not limited to, notification of the appropriate personnel, creating an audit record, and rejecting invalid input.\n\nThis calls for inspection of application source code, which will require collaboration with the application developers. It is recognized that in many cases, the database administrator (DBA) is organizationally separate from the application developers, and may have limited, if any, access to source code. Nevertheless, protections of this type are so important to the secure operation of databases that they must not be ignored. At a minimum, the DBA must attempt to obtain assurances from the development organization that this issue has been addressed, and must document what has been discovered.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72613",
            "V-58183"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002754"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "1fe90e13-4bb8-5d30-88d3-58279a7b124d",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL supplies process-isolated backends, transactional/error semantics and authenticated connections; UDS restricts the container, and application input/error handling is explicit. Failed startup does not grant an authenticated SQL session.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-roles] pg-roles\nPostgreSQL role attributes govern LOGIN, privilege grants and connection limits; default per-role connection limit is unlimited. Superuser bypasses permission checks.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/role-attributes.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[app-error] app-error\nUnknown/DB errors map to generic 500 problem details; HTTP transport and internal NATS dispatcher use the same mapper. Explicit application exceptions retain their supplied detail.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/errors/problem.mapper.ts\n\n[app-validation] app-validation\nGlobal interceptor validates declared params/query/headers/JSON with Zod, enforces Content-Type, and fails routes missing a documented exemption/schema; binary/multipart paths are handled separately.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/3f3b51d454197c077b33896caf93de72b94eafda/apps/api/src/common/openapi/openapi.validation.interceptor.ts",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL supplies process-isolated backends, transactional/error semantics and authenticated connections; UDS restricts the container, and application input/error handling is explicit. Failed startup does not grant an authenticated SQL session.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206610",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206610",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000454",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206610",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000454",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206610r961677_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206610r961677",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000454-DB-000389",
          "rule_title": "When updates are applied to the DBMS software, any software components that have been replaced or made unnecessary must be removed.",
          "fix_text": "Identify and remove software components that have been replaced or made unnecessary.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If software components that have been replaced or made unnecessary are not removed, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Previous versions of DBMS components that are not removed from the information system after updates have been installed may be exploited by adversaries. \n\nSome DBMSs' installation tools may remove older versions of software automatically from the information system. In other cases, manual review and removal will be required. In planning installations and upgrades, organizations must include steps (automated, manual, or both) to identify and remove the outdated modules.\n\nA transition period may be necessary when both the old and the new software are required. This should be taken into account in the planning.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72605",
            "V-58175"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002617"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "44d72b00-c1fb-5b47-9497-1384e64eaa45",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: DB software is delivered as an image/release artifact with verified source digests and package provenance; replacement image deployments replace software layers. Approval of trust/signing identities and old PVC/file cleanup must remain in the release procedure.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py\n\n[app-namespace] app-namespace\nAll declared workload charts target horizon-uds; package supplies dedicated namespace, distinct workload charts and generated deployment credentials.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. DB software is delivered as an image/release artifact with verified source digests and package provenance; replacement image deployments replace software layers. Approval of trust/signing identities and old PVC/file cleanup must remain in the release procedure.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206611",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206611",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000456",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206611",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000456",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206611r1137667_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206611r1137667",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000456-DB-000390",
          "rule_title": "Security-relevant software updates to the DBMS must be installed within 30 days unless the time period is directed by an authoritative source (e.g., IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, and STIGs).",
          "fix_text": "Install security-relevant software updates to the DBMS within 30 days unless the time period is directed by an authoritative source.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify security-relevant software updates to the DBMS are installed within 30 days unless the time period is directed by an authoritative source (e.g., IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, and STIGs).\n\nIf security-relevant software updates to the DBMS are not installed within 30 days unless the time period is directed by an authoritative source, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Security flaws with software applications, including database management systems, are discovered daily. Vendors are constantly updating and patching their products to address newly discovered security vulnerabilities. Organizations (including any contractor to the organization) are required to promptly install security-relevant software updates (e.g., patches, service packs, and hot fixes). Flaws discovered during security assessments, continuous monitoring, incident response activities, or information system error handling must also be addressed expeditiously. \n\nOrganization-defined time periods for updating security-relevant software may vary based on a variety of factors including, for example, the security category of the information system or the criticality of the update (i.e., severity of the vulnerability related to the discovered flaw). \n\nThis requirement will apply to software patch management solutions used to install patches across the enclave and to applications themselves that are not part of that patch management solution. For example, many browsers today provide the capability to install their own patch software. Patch criticality, as well as system criticality, will vary. Therefore, the tactical situations regarding the patch management process will also vary. This means that the time period utilized must be a configurable parameter. Time frames for application of security-relevant software updates may be dependent upon the Information Assurance Vulnerability Management (IAVM) process.\n\nThe application will be configured to check for and install security-relevant software updates within an identified time period from the availability of the update. The specific time period will be defined by an authoritative source (e.g., IAVM, CTOs, DTMs, and STIGs).",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "V-58177",
            "SV-72607"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002605"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "a7169d24-b009-5e47-8d6f-550d0943cb70",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The candidate source builds PostgreSQL18 and the release tooling pins/verifies image identities. That major version is vendor-supported per PostgreSQL's supported-version documentation; update timeliness remains an ongoing release-management responsibility.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-password] pg-password\nPostgreSQL18 defaults password_encryption to SCRAM-SHA-256. Existing persistent data/configuration can override defaults; generated new credentials and selected image must be distinguished from existing volumes.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-connection.html\n\n[images] Image release process\nBuilder verifies source digests and produces archive hashes; sample values with empty digests do not establish that a built release is unpinned. Assessed release/SBOM/scans still needed.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/scripts/build-bundle.py",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The candidate source builds PostgreSQL18 and the release tooling pins/verifies image identities. That major version is vendor-supported per PostgreSQL's supported-version documentation; update timeliness remains an ongoing release-management responsibility.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206612",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206612",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000492",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206612",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000492",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206612r961791_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206612r961791",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000492-DB-000332",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must be able to generate audit records when security objects are accessed.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, are accessed.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, are accessed, to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS architecture makes it impossible for any user, even with the highest privileges, to directly view or directly modify the contents of its built-in security objects, and if there are no additional, locally-defined security objects in the database(s), this is not a finding.\n\nReview DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, are accessed, to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, are accessed, to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Changes to the security configuration must be tracked.\n\nThis requirement applies to situations where security data is retrieved or modified via data manipulation operations, as opposed to via specialized security functionality.\n\nIn an SQL environment, types of access include, but are not necessarily limited to:\nSELECT\nINSERT\nUPDATE\nDELETE\nEXECUTE",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72513",
            "V-58083"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "414ecaa9-86e9-5eaa-9c6c-e776abd45515",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: pgAudit can audit security-object access, but source selects no corresponding classes/object policy. Application permission/feature-flag records are useful partial coverage; they do not audit every successful read/change/execution of native security objects. This exact check includes actual event generation, not capability alone.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Enable targeted native security-object audit selection or establish an accepted equivalent event mapping.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-extension] re-database-extension\nMigration creates pgAudit extension but catches unavailable errors; no session/object audit event settings are enabled by this migration.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[re-database-pgaudit] re-database-pgaudit\npgAudit supports selectable session/object events and role/DDL/read/write classes; preloading/extension creation alone does not select classes. Default session audit setting is none.\nhttps://github.com/pgaudit/pgaudit/blob/REL_18_STABLE/README.md\n\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-permission-audit] re-database-permission-audit\nAppend-only role-change records carry tenant, individual actor/subject, grant/revoke action and timestamp; database-specific admin auditing remains distinct.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "pgAudit can audit security-object access, but source selects no corresponding classes/object policy. Application permission/feature-flag records are useful partial coverage; they do not audit every successful read/change/execution of native security objects. This exact check includes actual event generation, not capability alone.\n\nNext action: Enable targeted native security-object audit selection or establish an accepted equivalent event mapping."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206613",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206613",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000492",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206613",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000492",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206613r961791_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206613r961791",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000492-DB-000333",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records when unsuccessful attempts to access security objects occur.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when it denies or fails to complete access to security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when it denies access to security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, such access to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when other errors prevent access to security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, such access to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS architecture makes it impossible for any user, even with the highest privileges, to directly view or directly modify the contents of its built-in security objects, and if there are no additional, locally-defined security objects in the database(s), this is not a finding.\n\nReview DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when the system denies or fails to complete attempts to access security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, such access to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when the system denies attempts to access security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, such access to include reads, creations, modifications and deletions of data, and execution of logic.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when other errors prevent attempts to access security object.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Changes to the security configuration must be tracked.\n\nThis requirement applies to situations where security data is retrieved or modified via data manipulation operations, as opposed to via specialized security functionality.\n\nIn an SQL environment, types of access include, but are not necessarily limited to:\nSELECT\nINSERT\nUPDATE\nDELETE\nEXECUTE\n\nTo aid in diagnosis, it is necessary to keep track of failed attempts in addition to the successful ones.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72515",
            "V-58085"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "e37f62b3-0e9e-564b-8bcd-9291352ab938",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206614",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206614",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000494",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206614",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000494",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206614r961797_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206614r961797",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000494-DB-000344",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records when categories of information (e.g., classification levels/security levels) are accessed.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when categories of information are accessed.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when categories of information are accessed, to include reads, creations, modifications, and deletions.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when categories of information are accessed, to include reads, creations, modifications, and deletions.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when categories of information are accessed, to include reads, creations, modifications, and deletions.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Changes in categories of information must be tracked. Without an audit trail, unauthorized access to protected data could go undetected.\n\nFor detailed information on categorizing information, refer to FIPS Publication 199, Standards for Security Categorization of Federal Information and Information Systems, and FIPS Publication 200, Minimum Security Requirements for Federal Information and Information Systems.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
