          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when it denies or fails to complete attempts to modify privileges/permissions/role membership.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when it denies attempts to modify privileges/permissions/role membership.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when other errors prevent attempts to modify privileges/permissions/role membership.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If there is no distinction in the DBMS's security architecture between modifying permissions on the one hand, and adding and deleting permissions on the other hand, this is not a finding.\n\nReview DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when the system denies or fails to complete attempts to modify privileges/permissions/role membership.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when the system denies attempts to modify privileges/permissions/role membership.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when other errors prevent attempts to modify privileges/permissions/role membership.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Failed attempts to change the permissions, privileges, and roles granted to users and roles must be tracked. Without an audit trail, unauthorized attempts to elevate or restrict privileges could go undetected. \n\nIn an SQL environment, modifying permissions is typically done via the GRANT, REVOKE, and DENY commands. \n\nTo aid in diagnosis, it is necessary to keep track of failed attempts in addition to the successful ones.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72507",
            "V-58077"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "4e574f57-1d58-59aa-8738-8e737d4a351a",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206620",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206620",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000496",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206620",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000496",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206620r961803_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206620r961803",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000496-DB-000334",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records when security objects are modified.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, are modified.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when security objects, such as tables, views, procedures, and functions, are modified.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS architecture makes it impossible for any user, even with the highest privileges, to modify the structure and logic of its built-in security objects, and if there are no additional, locally-defined security objects in the database(s), this is not a finding.\n\nReview DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when security objects are modified.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when security objects are modified.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Changes in the database objects (tables, views, procedures, functions) that record and control permissions, privileges, and roles granted to users and roles must be tracked. Without an audit trail, unauthorized changes to the security subsystem could go undetected. The database could be severely compromised or rendered inoperative.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72517",
            "V-58087"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "54488b17-74eb-58b5-a3fe-26e4916c2053",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL built-in catalogs are protected, but the application also defines local security objects (roles, RLS policies and authorization/audit functions). Thus the built-in-only exemption does not cover the entire product; successful changes/drops of those objects still need selected native or equivalent audit records.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Select DDL/ROLE security-object audit events or attach an equivalent privileged deployment/change trail with required event fields.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-rls] re-database-rls\nRLS predicates bind rows to transaction tenant context; tenant labels are separate from the persisted classification labels.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/rls_policies.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-flag-audit] re-database-flag-audit\nSecurity-definer feature flag audit triggers record actor from app.user_id, action/old/new values and reason; demonstrates application/database-trigger auditing can meet a defined event requirement without pgAudit for that event.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/feature_flag_functions.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-pgaudit] re-database-pgaudit\npgAudit supports selectable session/object events and role/DDL/read/write classes; preloading/extension creation alone does not select classes. Default session audit setting is none.\nhttps://github.com/pgaudit/pgaudit/blob/REL_18_STABLE/README.md\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "PostgreSQL built-in catalogs are protected, but the application also defines local security objects (roles, RLS policies and authorization/audit functions). Thus the built-in-only exemption does not cover the entire product; successful changes/drops of those objects still need selected native or equivalent audit records.\n\nNext action: Select DDL/ROLE security-object audit events or attach an equivalent privileged deployment/change trail with required event fields."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206621",
