          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Classification categories are real product fields, not an unspecified nonexistent taxonomy. Files metadata can be read/reclassified/deleted, but those code paths do not emit a complete actor/object/old-new/outcome category audit trail, and native pgAudit classes are unset. For denials, RLS invisibility/404 is not always a PostgreSQL ERROR. Reuse one application/DB audit integration instead of implementing a second classification model.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Add or evidence category-specific read/change/delete success/denial events in the shared application/DB audit stream; preserve individual actor and classification context.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-labels] re-database-labels\nActual product taxonomy exists: classification_level enum unclassified/classified/secret/top_secret; files.classification persists labels.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/files.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-label-writes] re-database-label-writes\nMetadata update/replace writes classification via repository and returns resulting row; no independent append-only classification-change audit event is emitted here. Ordinary access/status logging is not a before/after label audit.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/files/files.service.ts\n\n[re-database-label-transport] re-database-label-transport\nTyped request/response schemas expose, validate and return classification values; labels remain bound to returned file objects rather than being dropped on transit.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/api/files/files.schema.ts\n\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-pg-log] re-database-pg-log\nPostgreSQL emits server errors to stderr by default; ERROR severity identifies failed/denied SQL and log_min_error_statement includes the failing command. Successful connection/disconnection logging requires explicit settings.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Classification categories are real product fields, not an unspecified nonexistent taxonomy. Files metadata can be read/reclassified/deleted, but those code paths do not emit a complete actor/object/old-new/outcome category audit trail, and native pgAudit classes are unset. For denials, RLS invisibility/404 is not always a PostgreSQL ERROR. Reuse one application/DB audit integration instead of implementing a second classification model.\n\nNext action: Add or evidence category-specific read/change/delete success/denial events in the shared application/DB audit stream; preserve individual actor and classification context."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206624",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206624",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000499",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206624",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000499",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206624r961812_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206624r961812",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000499-DB-000330",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records when privileges/permissions are deleted.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when privileges/permissions/role memberships are removed, revoked, or denied to any user or role.\n\nConfigure DBMS audit settings to generate an audit record when privileges/permissions/role memberships are removed, revoked, or denied to any user or role.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when privileges/permissions/role memberships are removed, revoked, or denied to any user or role.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when privileges/permissions/role memberships are removed, revoked, or denied to any user or role.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Changes in the permissions, privileges, and roles granted to users and roles must be tracked. Without an audit trail, unauthorized elevation or restriction of privileges could go undetected. Elevated privileges give users access to information and functionality that they should not have; restricted privileges wrongly deny access to authorized users.\n\nIn an SQL environment, deleting permissions is typically done via the REVOKE or DENY command.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72509",
            "V-58079"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "6262b910-04d3-5a40-a5bb-f29caaad3c0c",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Product permission grant/revoke actions already have append-only actor audit records, but this exact DBMS control also covers native SQL role/privilege changes. Those native successful GRANT/REVOKE operations are not selected for logging by current pgAudit configuration. Do not claim the whole product has no privilege audit, and do not count duplicate native event tasks separately.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Enable native ROLE/privilege event selection or retain accepted equivalent administrative change records; keep application records as existing coverage.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-permission-audit] re-database-permission-audit\nAppend-only role-change records carry tenant, individual actor/subject, grant/revoke action and timestamp; database-specific admin auditing remains distinct.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/permission_audit.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-pgaudit] re-database-pgaudit\npgAudit supports selectable session/object events and role/DDL/read/write classes; preloading/extension creation alone does not select classes. Default session audit setting is none.\nhttps://github.com/pgaudit/pgaudit/blob/REL_18_STABLE/README.md\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Product permission grant/revoke actions already have append-only actor audit records, but this exact DBMS control also covers native SQL role/privilege changes. Those native successful GRANT/REVOKE operations are not selected for logging by current pgAudit configuration. Do not claim the whole product has no privilege audit, and do not count duplicate native event tasks separately.\n\nNext action: Enable native ROLE/privilege event selection or retain accepted equivalent administrative change records; keep application records as existing coverage."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206625",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206625",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000499",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206625",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000499",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206625r961812_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206625r961812",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000499-DB-000331",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records when unsuccessful attempts to delete privileges/permissions occur.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when it denies or fails to complete attempts to remove, revoke, or deny privileges/permissions/role membership to any user or role.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when it denies attempts to remove, revoke, or deny privileges/permissions/role membership to any user or role.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when other errors prevent attempts to remove, revoke, or deny privileges/permissions/role membership to any user or role.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when the system denies or fails to complete attempts remove, revoke, or deny privileges/permissions/role membership to any user or role.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when the system denies attempts to remove, revoke, or deny privileges/permissions/role membership to any user or role.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when other errors prevent attempts to remove, revoke, or deny privileges/permissions/role membership to any user or role.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Failed attempts to change the permissions, privileges, and roles granted to users and roles must be tracked. Without an audit trail, unauthorized attempts to elevate or restrict privileges could go undetected. \n\nIn an SQL environment, deleting permissions is typically done via the REVOKE or DENY command. \n\nTo aid in diagnosis, it is necessary to keep track of failed attempts in addition to the successful ones.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72511",
            "V-58081"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "5e5b84da-0528-5aae-ae09-f3b6c7fe65b0",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206626",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206626",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000501",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206626",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000501",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206626r961818_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206626r961818",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000501-DB-000336",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records when security objects are deleted.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when security objects are deleted.\n\nConfigure the DBMS to produce audit records when security objects are deleted.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS architecture makes it impossible for any user, even with the highest privileges, to drop its built-in security objects, and if there are no additional, locally-defined security objects in the database(s), this is not a finding.\n\nReview DBMS documentation to verify that audit records can be produced when security objects are drop.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit configurations to verify that audit records are produced when security objects are drop.\n\nIf they are not produced, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The removal of security objects from the database/DBMS would seriously degrade a system's information assurance posture. If such an event occurs, it must be logged.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72521",
            "V-58091"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "6476c1dc-917e-5a11-a729-c2ab97598167",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL built-in catalogs are protected, but the application also defines local security objects (roles, RLS policies and authorization/audit functions). Thus the built-in-only exemption does not cover the entire product; successful changes/drops of those objects still need selected native or equivalent audit records.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Select DDL/ROLE security-object audit events or attach an equivalent privileged deployment/change trail with required event fields.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-rls] re-database-rls\nRLS predicates bind rows to transaction tenant context; tenant labels are separate from the persisted classification labels.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/rls_policies.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-flag-audit] re-database-flag-audit\nSecurity-definer feature flag audit triggers record actor from app.user_id, action/old/new values and reason; demonstrates application/database-trigger auditing can meet a defined event requirement without pgAudit for that event.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/schema/feature_flag_functions.pg.hcl\n\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-pgaudit] re-database-pgaudit\npgAudit supports selectable session/object events and role/DDL/read/write classes; preloading/extension creation alone does not select classes. Default session audit setting is none.\nhttps://github.com/pgaudit/pgaudit/blob/REL_18_STABLE/README.md\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "PostgreSQL built-in catalogs are protected, but the application also defines local security objects (roles, RLS policies and authorization/audit functions). Thus the built-in-only exemption does not cover the entire product; successful changes/drops of those objects still need selected native or equivalent audit records.\n\nNext action: Select DDL/ROLE security-object audit events or attach an equivalent privileged deployment/change trail with required event fields."
        },
