          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS capable of producing the required audit records when object access occurs.\n\nConfigure audit settings to create audit records when the specified access to the specified objects is unsuccessfully attempted.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review DBMS documentation to verify that administrative users can specify database objects for which access must be audited, and can specify which kinds of access must be audited.\n\nIf the DBMS is not capable of this, this is a finding.\n\nReview DBMS documentation to determine whether the application owner has specified database objects (tables, views, procedures, functions, etc.) for which access must be audited.\n\nReview the DBMS/database security and audit settings to verify that audit records are created for unsuccessful attempts at the specified access to the specified objects.\n\nIf not, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Without tracking all or selected types of access to all or selected objects (tables, views, procedures, functions, etc.), it would be difficult to establish, correlate, and investigate the events relating to an incident or identify those responsible for one. \n\nIn an SQL environment, types of access include, but are not necessarily limited to:\nSELECT\nINSERT\nUPDATE\nDELETE\nEXECUTE\n\nTo aid in diagnosis, it is necessary to keep track of failed attempts in addition to the successful ones.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72551",
            "V-58121"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "e0448f9e-1f78-5276-b3a9-3cf5c525db3f",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. PostgreSQL natively logs authentication and permission/error failures, while the standard server-log path is collected by Vector. This supports existence of failure events; full actor/source/content and successful-event coverage remain explicitly flagged under database-auditing.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206638",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206638",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000508",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206638",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000508",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206638r961839_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206638r961839",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000508-DB-000358",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must generate audit records for all direct access to the database(s).",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to generate audit records for all direct access to the database(s).",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS does not generate audit records for all direct access to the database(s), this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "In this context, direct access is any query, command, or call to the DBMS that comes from any source other than the application(s) that it supports. Examples would be the command line or a database management utility program. The intent is to capture all activity from administrative and non-standard sources.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72553",
            "V-58123"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-000172"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "9610aa96-23e4-5681-b626-b29fdb20de96",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The delivered code preloads pgAudit but does not select session event classes or enable successful native connection/disconnection records. Vector centralizes emitted logs; optional SQL tracing defaults off and cannot account for direct administrator connections. Therefore the specific required native DB event coverage remains a real shared configuration gap, despite existing product-level audit mechanisms.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Enable the required native connection/disconnection/privileged/direct-access event selection and contextual fields, with data/performance/redaction review; one shared audit workstream covers these rows.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-extension] re-database-extension\nMigration creates pgAudit extension but catches unavailable errors; no session/object audit event settings are enabled by this migration.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000002_create_extensions.sql\n\n[re-database-tracing] re-database-tracing\nOptional SQL tracing carries parameterized query identity, db.namespace and outcome, excluding bind values. Config defaults tracing off, so it is not an enabled universal SQL audit equivalent.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/observability/database-telemetry.ts\n\n[re-database-config] re-database-config\nOpenTelemetry tracing defaults false, and SQL SSL default disabled under intended ambient mesh. No native DB certificate-auth/cache mechanism is configured.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/apps/api/src/core/config/config.schema.ts\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-database-pg-log] re-database-pg-log\nPostgreSQL emits server errors to stderr by default; ERROR severity identifies failed/denied SQL and log_min_error_statement includes the failing command. Successful connection/disconnection logging requires explicit settings.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "The delivered code preloads pgAudit but does not select session event classes or enable successful native connection/disconnection records. Vector centralizes emitted logs; optional SQL tracing defaults off and cannot account for direct administrator connections. Therefore the specific required native DB event coverage remains a real shared configuration gap, despite existing product-level audit mechanisms.\n\nNext action: Enable the required native connection/disconnection/privileged/direct-access event selection and contextual fields, with data/performance/redaction review; one shared audit workstream covers these rows."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206639",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206639",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000514",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206639",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000514",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206639r1137660_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206639r1137660",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000514-DB-000381",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must implement NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules to provision digital signatures.",
          "fix_text": "Implement NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules to provision digital signatures.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS does not employ NIST FIPS 140-2 or 140-3 validated cryptographic modules to provision digital signatures, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Use of weak or untested encryption algorithms undermines the purposes of utilizing encryption to protect data. The application must implement cryptographic modules adhering to the higher standards approved by the federal government since this provides assurance they have been tested and validated.\n\nFor detailed information, refer to NIST FIPS Publication 140-3, Security Requirements For Cryptographic Modules. Note that the product's cryptographic modules must be validated and certified by NIST as FIPS-compliant.\n\nThis requirement also applies to Zero Trust initiatives.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72589",
            "V-58159"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "02ccc52f-a872-5cdd-af29-993059a1abdb",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Exact selected PostgreSQL image provenance and actual build SBOM identify PostgreSQL18.6-r7, CMVP5132 OpenSSL3.4.0-r5 and approved-only fipshardened configuration. Custom extension staging preserves runtime libcrypto/libssl instead of replacing them. This resolves DBMS cryptographic-module selection for its crypto/signature/hash/confidentiality operations; it does not assert that an unspecified PVC backend has encryption enabled or that the DBMS implements an application signing workflow.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the exact DB image/module certificate mapping; handle backend at-rest encryption selection and any unused signing-function applicability in the separate storage/component assessment.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-compose] re-images-compose\nActual selected source pins node-fips24.22.1-slim@b61e57c2..., nginx-fips1.31.8@72d1e862..., postgres-fips18.6@9760ae95..., python-fips3.13.18; release runtimeSource binds exact platform manifests and PostgreSQL source/build definition.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/compose.yml\n\n[re-images-pg-sbom] re-images-pg-sbom\nActual selected PostgreSQL build source9d3725b/run41699186459 SBOM contains postgresql18.6-r7, NIST-CMVP-5132, OpenSSL FIPS provider3.4.0-r5 and fipshardened configuration. The multistage extension copy deliberately excludes/reuses runtime libcrypto/libssl, preserving base module. Release verifies selected PostgreSQL definition/context hashes and exact platform digest3431665e....\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/41699186459/artifacts/13263507123\n\n[re-images-vendor] re-images-vendor\nVendor documents FIPS images configured in approved-only mode; using these images is concrete enabled configuration, not an unconfigured capability. FIPS apps do not require host kernel FIPS because validated user-space entropy is included.\nhttps://www.chainguard.dev/docs/platform/fips/faqs\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Exact selected PostgreSQL image provenance and actual build SBOM identify PostgreSQL18.6-r7, CMVP5132 OpenSSL3.4.0-r5 and approved-only fipshardened configuration. Custom extension staging preserves runtime libcrypto/libssl instead of replacing them. This resolves DBMS cryptographic-module selection for its crypto/signature/hash/confidentiality operations; it does not assert that an unspecified PVC backend has encryption enabled or that the DBMS implements an application signing workflow.\n\nNext action: Retain the exact DB image/module certificate mapping; handle backend at-rest encryption selection and any unused signing-function applicability in the separate storage/component assessment."
        },
