          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Exact selected PostgreSQL image provenance and actual build SBOM identify PostgreSQL18.6-r7, CMVP5132 OpenSSL3.4.0-r5 and approved-only fipshardened configuration. Custom extension staging preserves runtime libcrypto/libssl instead of replacing them. This resolves DBMS cryptographic-module selection for its crypto/signature/hash/confidentiality operations; it does not assert that an unspecified PVC backend has encryption enabled or that the DBMS implements an application signing workflow.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Retain the exact DB image/module certificate mapping; handle backend at-rest encryption selection and any unused signing-function applicability in the separate storage/component assessment.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-images-release] re-images-release\nSuccessful Nov5 trusted release2026.45.4-uds.1 binds Horizonb84b9af/build19710/run42109769948, package d528b7f, Core1.14.0, actual identity.mode=uds issuer https://sso.uds.dev/realms/uds. Twelve delivered image platform digests are explicit; no bundled Keycloak is shipped. Published package digest sha256:486f55939f94ab081d2a12ecade5439512f8f7c4b3119c40b027c711d386adc9. Read build-info/platform-contract/publication metadata artifact13464284834, not just example chart defaults.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-images-compose] re-images-compose\nActual selected source pins node-fips24.22.1-slim@b61e57c2..., nginx-fips1.31.8@72d1e862..., postgres-fips18.6@9760ae95..., python-fips3.13.18; release runtimeSource binds exact platform manifests and PostgreSQL source/build definition.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/compose.yml\n\n[re-images-pg-sbom] re-images-pg-sbom\nActual selected PostgreSQL build source9d3725b/run41699186459 SBOM contains postgresql18.6-r7, NIST-CMVP-5132, OpenSSL FIPS provider3.4.0-r5 and fipshardened configuration. The multistage extension copy deliberately excludes/reuses runtime libcrypto/libssl, preserving base module. Release verifies selected PostgreSQL definition/context hashes and exact platform digest3431665e....\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/actions/runs/41699186459/artifacts/13263507123\n\n[re-images-vendor] re-images-vendor\nVendor documents FIPS images configured in approved-only mode; using these images is concrete enabled configuration, not an unconfigured capability. FIPS apps do not require host kernel FIPS because validated user-space entropy is included.\nhttps://www.chainguard.dev/docs/platform/fips/faqs\n\n[re-images-nist5132] re-images-nist5132\nNIST lists Chainguard FIPS Provider for OpenSSL, FIPS140-3 certificate5132, Active, sunset2031-01-13, approved-mode caveat. Exact API/web/Postgres SBOM markers map to this certificate; no certificate number guessed from generic FIPS naming.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/5132",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Exact selected PostgreSQL image provenance and actual build SBOM identify PostgreSQL18.6-r7, CMVP5132 OpenSSL3.4.0-r5 and approved-only fipshardened configuration. Custom extension staging preserves runtime libcrypto/libssl instead of replacing them. This resolves DBMS cryptographic-module selection for its crypto/signature/hash/confidentiality operations; it does not assert that an unspecified PVC backend has encryption enabled or that the DBMS implements an application signing workflow.\n\nNext action: Retain the exact DB image/module certificate mapping; handle backend at-rest encryption selection and any unused signing-function applicability in the separate storage/component assessment."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206642",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-206642",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000515",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-206642",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000515",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-206642r961860_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-206642r961860",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000515-DB-000318",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must off-load audit data to a separate log management facility; this shall be continuous and in near real time for systems with a network connection to the storage facility and weekly or more often for stand-alone systems.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS or deploy and configure software tools to transfer audit records to a centralized log management system, continuously and in near-real time where a continuous network connection to the log management system exists, or at least weekly in the absence of such a connection.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Review the system documentation for a description of how audit records are off-loaded.\n\nIf the DBMS has a continuous network connection to the centralized log management system, but the DBMS audit records are not written directly to the centralized log management system or transferred in near-real-time, this is a finding.\n\nIf the DBMS does not have a continuous network connection to the centralized log management system, and the DBMS audit records are not transferred to the centralized log management system weekly or more often, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Information stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration.\n\nOff-loading is a common process in information systems with limited audit storage capacity. \n\nThe DBMS may write audit records to database tables, to files in the file system, to other kinds of local repository, or directly to a centralized log management system. Whatever the method used, it must be compatible with off-loading the records to the centralized system.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72485",
            "V-58055"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-001851"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "64654a02-cf28-5d3c-b575-d94e1936b474",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Vector continuously collects container stderr and ships it with buffering/acknowledgement to Loki; Grafana centrally queries it. The transport/aggregation path is implemented even though database-audit event selection requires configuration.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-vector] core-vector\nVector Agent collects Kubernetes pod and node log files, labels namespace/container/host, uses acknowledged disk buffers, and ships continuously to Loki; metrics are exported.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[core-loki] core-loki\nLoki uses ambient mesh; ingress is limited to Grafana, Vector, FalcoSidekick and monitoring service identities; object-store egress is declared separately.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/loki/chart/templates/uds-package.yaml\n\n[core-grafana] core-grafana\nGrafana disables local login, uses UDS OAuth, maps Admin group to Admin and Auditor to Viewer, and rejects unmatched groups. OAuth secrets are file-mounted.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml\n\n[pg-logging] pg-logging\nPostgreSQL18 defaults connection logging disabled and disconnection logging off; failed authentication is always logged. stderr is the default server-log destination.\nhttps://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Vector continuously collects container stderr and ships it with buffering/acknowledgement to Loki; Grafana centrally queries it. The transport/aggregation path is implemented even though database-audit event selection requires configuration.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-206643",
