          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "6406638f-db30-5567-ad06-acd4aba59363",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Native session limits and selected native audit events require configuration. This roll-up stays linked to those verified shared tasks; it is not another implementation or a claim every DB requirement fails. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with partial coverage for this and similar umbrella hardening requirements. Verify component-specific control mappings, inherited protections and accountable ownership, then resolve or formally disposition the linked residuals. Treat this as a closure/mapping check, not a duplicate implementation project or a claim that every component control fails.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Verify the assessed component inventory and applicable hardening guidance, responsibility boundaries and accepted inherited-control mappings. Link the specific configuration, implementation and verification residuals already tracked in this assessment, complete or formally disposition them with evidence, and retain the component-specific checklist. Do not create duplicate remediation work for this umbrella requirement or treat source-level inherited recommendations as final accepted compliance.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-postgres-values] re-database-postgres-values\nPostgres chart args preload pgaudit and disable JIT; no pgaudit.log, native connection/disconnection logging, full log prefix, password policy hooks, session disconnect policy or encryption StorageClass selected. PVC uses host default StorageClass.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/values/postgres.yaml\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql\n\n[re-database-actual-image] re-database-actual-image\nStatic export of exact delivered PostgreSQL image confirms inherited entrypoint has no audit override, PostgreSQL18 sample defaults connection logging empty/disconnections off/prefix timestampPID; actual image has FIPS provider and enabled OpenSSL configuration. File hashes and SBOM details preserved in database-image-inspection.json.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Native session limits and selected native audit events require configuration. This roll-up stays linked to those verified shared tasks; it is not another implementation or a claim every DB requirement fails. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work with partial coverage for this and similar umbrella hardening requirements. Verify component-specific control mappings, inherited protections and accountable ownership, then resolve or formally disposition the linked residuals. Treat this as a closure/mapping check, not a duplicate implementation project or a claim that every component control fails.\n\nNext action: Verify the assessed component inventory and applicable hardening guidance, responsibility boundaries and accepted inherited-control mappings. Link the specific configuration, implementation and verification residuals already tracked in this assessment, complete or formally disposition them with evidence, and retain the component-specific checklist. Do not create duplicate remediation work for this umbrella requirement or treat source-level inherited recommendations as final accepted compliance."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-233495",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-233495",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000416",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-233495",
          "severity": "high",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000416",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-233495r1117186_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-233495r1117186",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000416-DB-000380",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must use NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information in accordance with the data owner's requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Deploy a DBMS compatible with the use of NSA-approved cryptography.\n\nConfigure the DBMS and related system components to use NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information in accordance with applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, and standards.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "If the DBMS is deployed in an unclassified environment, this is not applicable (NA).\n\nIf the DBMS is not configured to use NSA-approved cryptography to protect classified information in accordance with applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, and standards, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Use of weak or untested encryption algorithms undermines the purposes of utilizing encryption to protect data. The application must implement cryptographic modules adhering to the higher standards approved by the federal government since this provides assurance they have been tested and validated.\n\nIt is the responsibility of the data owner to assess the cryptography requirements in light of applicable federal laws, Executive Orders, directives, policies, regulations, and standards.\n\nNSA-approved cryptography for classified networks is hardware based. This requirement addresses the compatibility of a DBMS with the encryption devices.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [
            "SV-72587",
            "V-58157"
          ],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-002450"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "592d7b18-05c5-53bb-9548-f44c925a7d4e",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Unclear\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The classified-deployment rule concerns DBMS compatibility with NSA-approved encryption devices. PostgreSQL can use transparent platform protection, but the checked UDS/PostgreSQL/SeaweedFS products have no exact match in the reviewed public CSfC component categories. The release and available MPN architecture do not name the selected approved storage/network protection. That missing selection prevents a supported pass; it does not prove PostgreSQL requires replacement or its own NSA certificate.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin SRE/security\n\nNext action: Identify or select the actual NSA-approved protection for PostgreSQL storage and relevant network boundaries. Record the product/model, version and approved configuration covering the DB. Reuse existing MPN protection where present; add a new layer only where needed.\n\nEvidence:\n[db-nsa-rule-discussion] db-nsa-rule-discussion\nV233495 discussion explicitly addresses DBMS compatibility with NSA-approved encryption devices for classified networks. The fix permits the DBMS and related system components to supply the protection. The approval need not attach to the PostgreSQL container itself.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-stig/blob/main/mpn/benchmarks/U_Database_V4R6_SRG/benchmark.xml\n\n[postgres-delivered-crypto] postgres-delivered-crypto\nThe exact delivered PostgreSQL image inspection identifies NIST-CMVP-5132, openssl-provider-fips-3.4.0 and FIPS provider configuration. This establishes the delivered module identity, not NSA approval for its classified use.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-stig/blob/main/mpn/evidence/database-image-provenance.json\n\n[classified-crypto-definition] classified-crypto-definition\nNSA-approved cryptography includes an approved algorithm, an implementation approved for the relevant environment and supporting key management. A FIPS validation certificate by itself does not establish that full approval.\nhttps://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/NSA_approved_cryptography\n\n[nsa-storage-current-list] nsa-storage-current-list\nActive SoftwareFDE,HardwareFDE,FileEncryption and general-purposeOS categories were reviewed. Exact searches found no PostgreSQL,SeaweedFS,UDS,OpenSSL,Chainguard or Azure names. This is a bounded current-list result, not a search of every NSA approval mechanism.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Resources/Commercial-Solutions-for-Classified-Program/Components-List/\n\n[nsa-network-msc-boundary] nsa-network-msc-boundary\nMSC1.3 §4.1.1 places the Red network behind the inner encryption component; it permits unencrypted classified traffic there. §§4/4.2 protect same-security-level sites across untrusted transport using two independent nested IPsec/MACsec tunnels. This is a network-boundary architecture, not a mandate to insert two CSfC layers between every pair of pods within one authorized Red enclave.\nhttps://www.nsa.gov/Portals/75/documents/CSfC%20CP%20Updates%202026/(U)%20Multi-Site%20Connectivity%20Capability%20Package%20v1.3.0.pdf?ver=mJWYHsX8a0wSY6bOPdCrZA%3D%3D\n\n[nsa-mpn-notional-abd] nsa-mpn-notional-abd\nVisually inspected MPN ABDv1.1 dated2026-11-02. It prominently states notional architecture requiring validation, shows AKS, Azure Blob, AzureOpenAI, MPN firewall and CNAP/BCAP-equivalent access point, and identifies no specific encryptor/storage-encryption product or NSA approval. The document UNCLASSIFIED marking describes the diagram, not proof that deployed application data is unclassified.\nhttps://drive.google.com/file/d/1rOyMxUBWdnwfrpnv-gvCWyne0drA5iZ5/view\n\n[nsa-mpn-release-contract-boundary] nsa-mpn-release-contract-boundary\nDownloaded release platform-contract.json establishes UDS identity/Core version but does not select cluster OS, storage class, network encryptor, Type1 module or CSfC solution. Application package evidence cannot establish an absent underlying MPN protection inventory.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Unclear. The classified-deployment rule concerns DBMS compatibility with NSA-approved encryption devices. PostgreSQL can use transparent platform protection, but the checked UDS/PostgreSQL/SeaweedFS products have no exact match in the reviewed public CSfC component categories. The release and available MPN architecture do not name the selected approved storage/network protection. That missing selection prevents a supported pass; it does not prove PostgreSQL requires replacement or its own NSA certificate.\n\nNext action: Identify or select the actual NSA-approved protection for PostgreSQL storage and relevant network boundaries. Record the product/model, version and approved configuration covering the DB. Reuse existing MPN protection where present; add a new layer only where needed."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263602",
          "group_tree": [
