          "group_id": "V-263605",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000795",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263605r982485_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263605r982485",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000795-DB-000130",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the DBMS is configured to alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information.\n\nIf the DBMS is not configured to alert organization-defined personnel or roles upon detection of unauthorized access, modification, or deletion of audit information, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Audit information includes all information needed to successfully audit system activity, such as audit records, audit log settings, audit reports, and personally identifiable information. Audit logging tools are those programs and devices used to conduct system audit and logging activities. Protection of audit information focuses on technical protection and limits the ability to access and execute audit logging tools to authorized individuals. Physical protection of audit information is addressed by both media protection controls and physical and environmental protection controls.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-003831"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "c8b9f44a-8476-579a-828f-844ec2d26526",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "open",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs work\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides collector/storage monitoring and runtime detections, so no DB-local alert engine is inherently needed. However, the selected package source still does not configure the exact audit75% threshold and required real-time audit-failure/tamper routes to support roles. General metrics/detections alone do not identify a configured notification path. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Identify detection sources for unauthorized access, modification and deletion of audit information. Configure missing detection/alert rules and route notifications to organization-defined personnel or roles. Test each required event type and recipient delivery; general uptime or account-change rules do not establish tamper coverage. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-alerts] re-database-alerts\nCore enables general cluster/storage monitoring; candidate source does not add a DB audit-volume75% rule or specific audit-failure/tamper notification receiver.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/prometheus-stack/values/kube-prometheus-stack-values.yaml\n\n[re-database-vector] re-database-vector\nVector offloads container/node logs with workload labels to centralized Loki. Collection preserves emitted records; it does not create absent successful SQL events.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/vector/values/values.yaml\n\n[re-database-log-access] re-database-log-access\nUDS Grafana disables local login and maps identity groups to Admin/Viewer; protected logs can supply privileged review without DBMS-local audit dashboard.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/grafana/values/values.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Monitoring and logging capability is partially implemented. UDS provides collector/storage monitoring and runtime detections, so no DB-local alert engine is inherently needed. However, the selected package source still does not configure the exact audit75% threshold and required real-time audit-failure/tamper routes to support roles. General metrics/detections alone do not identify a configured notification path. Human review on 2026-11-10 confirmed Needs work under the instruction to apply the recommendation where existing capabilities need alerting configuration. Credit existing sources and monitoring; configure and verify the specific required alert coverage and recipient delivery. This does not establish that every required detector or alert rule is already present.\n\nNext action: Identify detection sources for unauthorized access, modification and deletion of audit information. Configure missing detection/alert rules and route notifications to organization-defined personnel or roles. Test each required event type and recipient delivery; general uptime or account-change rules do not establish tamper coverage. Retain event-source mappings, rules, routes and delivery-test evidence. Reuse shared monitoring rather than create a separate application alert engine."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263606",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263606",
