          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "5729145d-abc9-504d-a907-dbb49766a406",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual release delegates human identity to UDS Keycloak, which supports configured X509/OTP authentication; native SQL identities are noninteractive workload accounts inside authenticated mesh. A second interactive MFA challenge on each pooled SQL connection is not the human access control objective. Privileged platform/SQL administration is assigned to the MPN access boundary and must be included in inheritance.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach shared UDS human MFA policy and MPN privileged-operator access inheritance, including approved device strength; do not present machine service connections as interactive human accounts.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-mtls] re-database-mtls\nUDS root STRICT mesh policy authenticates/encrypts enrolled workload hops independently of PostgreSQL native TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[re-database-package-network] re-database-package-network\nUDS ambient mesh enrolls horizon namespace and controls SQL connectivity through namespace policy. Public exposes API/web/NATS and bundledKeycloak only when selected; no public PostgreSQL endpoint.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual release delegates human identity to UDS Keycloak, which supports configured X509/OTP authentication; native SQL identities are noninteractive workload accounts inside authenticated mesh. A second interactive MFA challenge on each pooled SQL connection is not the human access control objective. Privileged platform/SQL administration is assigned to the MPN access boundary and must be included in inheritance.\n\nNext action: Attach shared UDS human MFA policy and MPN privileged-operator access inheritance, including approved device strength; do not present machine service connections as interactive human accounts."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263609",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263609",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000825",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263609",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000825",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263609r982489_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263609r982489",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000825-DB-000180",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that the device meets organization-defined strength of mechanism requirements.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that the device meets organization-defined strength of mechanism requirements.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the DBMS is configured to implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that the device meets organization-defined strength of mechanism requirements.\n\nIf the DBMS is not configured to implement multifactor authentication for local; network; and/or remote access to privileged accounts; and/or nonprivileged accounts such that the device meets organization-defined strength of mechanism requirements, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "The purpose of requiring a device that is separate from the system to which the user is attempting to gain access for one of the factors during multifactor authentication is to reduce the likelihood of compromising authenticators or credentials stored on the system. Adversaries may be able to compromise such authenticators or credentials and subsequently impersonate authorized users. Implementing one of the factors on a separate device (e.g., a hardware token), provides a greater strength of mechanism and an increased level of assurance in the authentication process.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004047"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "c146e9ee-f738-5434-8360-110b0dcca409",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: The actual release delegates human identity to UDS Keycloak, which supports configured X509/OTP authentication; native SQL identities are noninteractive workload accounts inside authenticated mesh. A second interactive MFA challenge on each pooled SQL connection is not the human access control objective. Privileged platform/SQL administration is assigned to the MPN access boundary and must be included in inheritance.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Attach shared UDS human MFA policy and MPN privileged-operator access inheritance, including approved device strength; do not present machine service connections as interactive human accounts.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-mtls] re-database-mtls\nUDS root STRICT mesh policy authenticates/encrypts enrolled workload hops independently of PostgreSQL native TLS.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/istio/common/chart/templates/peer-authentication.yaml\n\n[re-database-package-network] re-database-package-network\nUDS ambient mesh enrolls horizon namespace and controls SQL connectivity through namespace policy. Public exposes API/web/NATS and bundledKeycloak only when selected; no public PostgreSQL endpoint.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-uds-package/templates/package.yaml",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. The actual release delegates human identity to UDS Keycloak, which supports configured X509/OTP authentication; native SQL identities are noninteractive workload accounts inside authenticated mesh. A second interactive MFA challenge on each pooled SQL connection is not the human access control objective. Privileged platform/SQL administration is assigned to the MPN access boundary and must be included in inheritance.\n\nNext action: Attach shared UDS human MFA policy and MPN privileged-operator access inheritance, including approved device strength; do not present machine service connections as interactive human accounts."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263610",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263610",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000830",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263610",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000830",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263610r981988_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263610r981988",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000830-DB-000190",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must, for password-based authentication, maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the DBMS is configured to maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency.\n\nIf the DBMS is not configured to maintain a list of commonly used, expected, or compromised passwords on an organization-defined frequency, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Password-based authentication applies to passwords regardless of whether they are used in single-factor or multifactor authentication. Long passwords or passphrases are preferable over shorter passwords. Enforced composition rules provide marginal security benefits while decreasing usability. However, organizations may choose to establish certain rules for password generation (e.g., minimum character length for long passwords) under certain circumstances and can enforce this requirement in IA-5(1)(h). Account recovery can occur, for example, in situations when a password is forgotten. Cryptographically protected passwords include salted one-way cryptographic hashes of passwords. The list of commonly used, compromised, or expected passwords includes passwords obtained from previous breach corpuses, dictionary words, and repetitive or sequential characters. The list includes context-specific words, such as the name of the service, username, and derivatives thereof.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
          "ia_controls": "",
          "responsibility": "",
          "mitigations": "",
          "mitigation_control": "",
          "legacy_ids": [],
          "ccis": [
            "CCI-004058"
          ],
          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "8f5e4f77-16f0-5c6b-a5a8-359f683610c8",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Needs N/A justification\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Only noninteractive deployment-generated DB secrets are selected; generation uses192-bit random values, not human dictionary choices, and users do not select their own native SQL passwords. Shared UDS provides human password policy. This supports machine-secret compensating-control tailoring rather than adding a duplicate human password blacklist UI to PostgreSQL; accepted rotation/compromise handling must still be recorded.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Document the approved machine-generated credential exemption/compensating control, including rotation on compromise; apply exact blacklist requirements if interactive native passwords are introduced.\n\nEvidence:\n[re-database-release] re-database-release\nSuccessful UDS push42114745685 packages2026.45.4-uds.1; platform-contract selects shared UDS identity/Core1.14.0, no bundled Keycloak image. Build-info identifies appb84b9af/build19710 and Postgres digest3431665ea7f0c2dfe4bef9673aac6221a58bdf910251857123b7fb363cbb030e from source9d3725b0/run41699186459.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/actions/runs/42114745685\n\n[re-database-machine-secrets] re-database-machine-secrets\nService/admin DB passwords generated with openssl rand -hex24 when not supplied, retained from existing Secrets on upgrade; app/openfga/admin are explicit deployment identities, not per-human accounts.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/zarf.yaml\n\n[re-database-role] re-database-role\nCreates/updates separate horizon_app LOGIN password role; grants DML/schema usage, no superuser or BYPASSRLS. No CONNECTION LIMIT, VALID UNTIL, password-check hook or SET ROLE escalation path.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon/blob/b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c/packages/database/migrations/00000000000003_provision_app_role.sql",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Needs N/A justification. Only noninteractive deployment-generated DB secrets are selected; generation uses192-bit random values, not human dictionary choices, and users do not select their own native SQL passwords. Shared UDS provides human password policy. This supports machine-secret compensating-control tailoring rather than adding a duplicate human password blacklist UI to PostgreSQL; accepted rotation/compromise handling must still be recorded.\n\nNext action: Document the approved machine-generated credential exemption/compensating control, including rotation on compromise; apply exact blacklist requirements if interactive native passwords are introduced."
        },
