          "reference_identifier": "2902",
          "uuid": "aea98f80-d485-5a39-868d-5efee7fb09d5",
          "stig_uuid": "20cc94ad-86d8-513a-b89a-6498204b9633",
          "status": "not_reviewed",
          "overrides": {},
          "comments": "Assessment recommendation: Likely covered\nEvidence state: source-inferred\nAssessed release: 2026.45.4-uds.1\nHorizon commit: b84b9afcd6983d553b6b8406317b6592ec90cb7c\n\nJustification: Database service is internal to the workload namespace and administrative access is through the separate MPN/UDS management plane; namespace/service identity controls provide logical separation. Native host access topology remains inherited.\n\nComponents: Delivered PostgreSQL service accounts and application database; UDS mesh and shared identity; no bundled Keycloak database\n\nOwner: Gallatin engineering/SRE (Horizon and self-managed UDS Core on MPN)\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control.\n\nEvidence:\n[core-network] core-network\nOperator generates namespace ingress/egress default-deny NetworkPolicy; connectivity requires declared allow rules.\nhttps://github.com/defenseunicorns/uds-core/blob/c15677633cffa17e99656a7d7ccd1f98d7cb6e4d/src/pepr/operator/controllers/network/defaults/default-deny-all.ts\n\n[app-db-values] app-db-values\nDB runs under UID70 with non-root/read-only/no-escalation and dedicated PGDATA PVC; default replica count is one. Only pgaudit preload is configured, not event classes.\nhttps://github.com/gallatinai/horizon-uds-bundle/blob/d528b7febf1ce8bb5ccf99661dade6ae09a84381/source/charts/horizon-postgres/values.yaml\n\n[host] MPN ownership\nCurrent scope assigns control plane/node OS to MPN; no host assessment or live configuration was supplied.\nhttps://gallatin.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GG/pages/733085804/WIP+STIG+SRG+Compliance+Scope+MPN",
          "finding_details": "Recommendation: Likely covered. Database service is internal to the workload namespace and administrative access is through the separate MPN/UDS management plane; namespace/service identity controls provide logical separation. Native host access topology remains inherited.\n\nNext action: Maintain the cited source/configuration mapping for the selected release and reassess any Gallatin deployment overrides that change this control."
        },
        {
          "group_id_src": "V-263619",
          "group_tree": [
            {
              "id": "V-263619",
              "title": "SRG-APP-000910",
              "description": "<GroupDescription></GroupDescription>"
            }
          ],
          "group_id": "V-263619",
          "severity": "medium",
          "group_title": "SRG-APP-000910",
          "rule_id_src": "SV-263619r982505_rule",
          "rule_id": "SV-263619r982505",
          "rule_version": "SRG-APP-000910-DB-000300",
          "rule_title": "The DBMS must include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.",
          "fix_text": "Configure the DBMS to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.",
          "weight": "10.0",
          "check_content": "Verify the DBMS is configured to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization.\n\nIf the DBMS is not configured to include only approved trust anchors in trust stores or certificate stores managed by the organization, this is a finding.",
          "check_content_ref": {
            "href": "Database_Generic_SRG.xml",
            "name": "M"
          },
          "classification": "Unclassified",
          "discussion": "Public key infrastructure (PKI) certificates are certificates with visibility external to organizational systems and certificates related to the internal operations of systems, such as application-specific time services. In cryptographic systems with a hierarchical structure, a trust anchor is an authoritative source (i.e., a certificate authority) for which trust is assumed and not derived. A root certificate for a PKI system is an example of a trust anchor. A trust store or certificate store maintains a list of trusted root certificates.",
          "false_positives": "",
          "false_negatives": "",
          "documentable": "false",
          "security_override_guidance": "",
          "potential_impacts": "",
          "third_party_tools": "",
